window.OPENBAND_HISTORY={"394": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Canoe Lake Cree First Nation is a Cree community in northwestern Saskatchewan whose land-based history predates Treaty 10. Hunting, fishing and trapping sustained community members across a broad traditional territory before and after treaty signing. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Our History\">[2]\u003c/a> On September 19, 1906, Chief John Iron and Headmen Baptiste Iron and Jerome Couilloneur signed Treaty 10 at Île-à-la-Crosse, according to the commissioner’s report, with the Crown’s commissioner, James Andrew Joseph McKenna. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a> The creation of the Primrose Lake Air Weapons Range in 1954 restricted access to approximately 75% of the Nation’s most productive traditional lands. A subsequent claim was settled in June 1997. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Our History\">[2]\u003c/a> These documented events describe part of the Nation’s treaty-era and later history; treaty records are not a complete account of its older Indigenous history or continuing Cree identity.\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty No. 10 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 19, 1906 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Île-à-la-Crosse, Saskatchewan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>John Iron \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts history-facts-secondary\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Headmen\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Baptiste Iron, Jerome Couilloneur \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty Commissioner\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>James Andrew Joseph McKenna \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://otc.ca/about/historical-biographies/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Historical Biographies\">[4]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cree (Nêhiyaw) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Our History\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Northwestern Saskatchewan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Our History\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land claim settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>June 1997 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/canoe_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canoe Lake First Nation\">[3]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>1954 · Primrose Lake Air Weapons Range\u003c/strong> — The range’s establishment removed access to about three quarters of the Nation’s most productive traditional lands, disrupting land-based livelihoods. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/canoe_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canoe Lake First Nation\">[3]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>1975–1997 · Claim and settlement\u003c/strong> — Canoe Lake submitted a claim in 1975 for the loss of traditional territory. Canada accepted it in 1995, and a settlement followed in June 1997. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/canoe_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canoe Lake First Nation\">[3]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">Signing location: McKenna’s contemporary report places the Canoe Lake band’s September 19 signing at Île-à-la-Crosse. A later OTC account lists Canoe Lake. The location above follows the contemporary report; the date is consistent across these accounts. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://otc.ca/otc-at-the-treaty-10-gathering/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: OTC at the Treaty 10 Gathering\">[8]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/english-river-dene-nation/\">English River First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Chief William Apisis signed Treaty 10 at Île-à-la-Crosse on August 28, 1906. The English River and Canoe Lake bands participated in the same 1906 treaty process. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://otc.ca/about/historical-biographies/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Historical Biographies\">[4]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>Clear Lake Band \u003cspan>Historical band\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Chief Raphael Bedshi-Dekkge signed Treaty 10 in 1906 for the historical Clear Lake Band, another participant in the original negotiations. Later known as the Peter Pond Band, it divided in 1972 into Buffalo River and Turnor Lake, now Birch Narrows Dene Nation. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/fra/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 · French transcription\">[7]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birch Narrows Dene Nation Community Plan\">[5]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo River Dene First Nation\">[9]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-related\">Present-day profiles: \u003ca href=\"/first-nations/birch-narrows-dene-nation/\">Birch Narrows Dene Nation\u003c/a> · \u003ca href=\"/first-nations/buffalo-river-dene-nation/\">Buffalo River Dene Nation\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These are shared historical treaty relationships. The historical Clear Lake Band is distinct from today’s Nations; these links do not imply common language, ancestry, governance or a political alliance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "406": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Ahtahkakoop’s treaty-era history is closely connected to Sandy Lake and the Green Lake Trail. Chief Ahtahkakoop signed Treaty 6 at Fort Carlton on August 23, 1876, seeking reserve land beside Mistawasis where his people already maintained homes and gardens. The 1878 survey did not provide the location or area requested. Agriculture became an important part of reserve life, but falling crop prices in 1929 and subsequent drought pushed some members to seek work elsewhere. These events describe one period of the community’s history, rather than the beginning of its people’s relationship with the land. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ahtahkakoop_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ahtahkakoop Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ahtahkakoop_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ahtahkakoop Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 23, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ahtahkakoop_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ahtahkakoop Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Carlton \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ahtahkakoop_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ahtahkakoop Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ahtahkakoop \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ahtahkakoop_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ahtahkakoop Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">Basil Starblanket succeeded Ahtahkakoop after his death in 1896. The Nation’s publishing company issued a substantial history of Ahtahkakoop and his people in 2000. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ahtahkakoop_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ahtahkakoop Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/mistawasis-nehiyawak/\">Mistawasis Nehiyawak\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The encyclopedia records Ahtahkakoop’s request for reserve land adjoining Mistawasis along the Green Lake Trail. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ahtahkakoop_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ahtahkakoop Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "369": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Beardy’s and Okemasis’ Cree Nation’s history includes hunting and trapping around Duck Lake before Treaty 6. Chief Beardy, also known as Kahmeeyistoowaysit, signed at Fort Carlton on August 28, 1876. He selected land west of Duck Lake for his people and those of Chief Okemasis, or Sayswaypus. Members built log homes and cultivated gardens there. A monument erected in 1953 commemorated lives lost during the 1885 Resistance. In 1991, the Nation and Saskatchewan Provincial Parks agreed to establish a tipi encampment at Fort Carlton, connecting the historic site with the community’s own interpretation of its past. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/beardys_and_okemasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beardy&#x27;s and Okemasis&#x27; Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/beardys_and_okemasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beardy&#x27;s and Okemasis&#x27; Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 28, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/beardys_and_okemasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beardy&#x27;s and Okemasis&#x27; Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Carlton \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/beardys_and_okemasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beardy&#x27;s and Okemasis&#x27; Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Beardy and Okemasis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/beardys_and_okemasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beardy&#x27;s and Okemasis&#x27; Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">Community justice initiatives later included sentencing and healing circles involving Elders. The Willow Cree Healing Lodge opened in 2003. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/beardys_and_okemasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beardy&#x27;s and Okemasis&#x27; Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "404": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Big River Cree First Nation’s history is rooted in the forests, lakes and waterways northwest of Prince Albert. Chief See-See-Way-um signed Treaty 6 on September 3, 1878. The encyclopedia describes the following two decades as a period when members supported themselves through hunting, trapping and fishing. Kenemotayoo succeeded him as chief, and a district trail carried his name. The area’s history also includes the Carlton Trail, an important fur-trade route between Edmonton and Winnipeg. These treaty and trade records describe particular historical encounters; they do not establish when Indigenous people first lived along the Big River. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/big_river_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Big River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/big_river_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Big River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 3, 1878 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/big_river_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Big River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>See-See-Way-um \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/big_river_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Big River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Big River district, Saskatchewan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/big_river_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Big River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "359": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Black Lake Denesuline First Nation’s ancestors joined Treaty 8 at Fond du Lac in July 1899 under Chief Maurice Piche, also known as Moberley. The people continued hunting, fishing and trapping over a much wider territory, including areas now within the Northwest Territories. The encyclopedia dates the formal division of Piche’s band into Black Lake and Fond du Lac bands to 1949. Black Lake was also historically called the Stony Rapids Band. Its reserves lie around Black Lake in northern Saskatchewan. The administrative division and treaty adhesion are milestones in the written record, rather than the origin of Denesuline occupation of the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/black_lake_denesuline_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black Lake Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/black_lake_denesuline_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black Lake Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/black_lake_denesuline_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black Lake Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Maurice Piche (Moberley) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/black_lake_denesuline_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black Lake Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Stony Rapids Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/black_lake_denesuline_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black Lake Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/fond-du-lac-denesuline-first-nation/\">Fond du Lac Denesuline First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Black Lake and Fond du Lac emerged from the formal division of Maurice Piche’s band, dated to 1949 in the encyclopedia. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/black_lake_denesuline_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black Lake Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "398": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Buffalo River Dene Nation’s treaty history is connected to the former Peter Pond Band and Treaty 10, signed on August 28, 1906. The encyclopedia associates the people with Buffalo Narrows before settlement at Buffalo River, while treating the earlier movement as a historical interpretation. The Peter Pond Band divided into Buffalo River and Turnor Lake in 1972; Turnor Lake is now Birch Narrows Dene Nation. Hunting and trapping around Watapi Lake were important livelihoods. Establishment of the Primrose Lake Air Weapons Range in 1954 restricted harvesting and travel, contributing to later claims for losses of land access and economic opportunities. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 10 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 28, 1906 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical band\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Peter Pond Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band division\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1972 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/birch-narrows-dene-nation/\">Birch Narrows Dene Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Buffalo River and Turnor Lake, now Birch Narrows, were formerly part of the Peter Pond Band and became separate bands in 1972. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "378": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Carry the Kettle Nakoda Nation’s documented reserve history includes a major relocation from the Cypress Hills region. After joining Treaty 4 on September 25, 1877, the people sought reserve land west of the Cypress Hills. In June 1882 they were moved toward Indian Head, and their reserve south of Sintaluta was surveyed in 1885. Historical leaders included Long Lodge, Man Who Took the Coat and Carry the Kettle, who became chief in 1891. Agriculture, livestock, hay production, craft work and wage labour supported families through the reserve period. This account concerns treaty-era administration and relocation, within a much older Nakoda history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/carry_the_kettle_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Carry the Kettle Nakoda Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/carry_the_kettle_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Carry the Kettle Nakoda Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 25, 1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/carry_the_kettle_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Carry the Kettle Nakoda Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1885 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/carry_the_kettle_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Carry the Kettle Nakoda Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Long Lodge; Man Who Took the Coat; Carry the Kettle \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/carry_the_kettle_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Carry the Kettle Nakoda Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "401": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Clearwater River Dene Nation’s history is tied to La Loche and the surrounding northern lakes. Historical accounts also use the names Big C and La Loche Band. Dene language and land-based work, especially fishing and trapping, are central features of the university’s account. The entry describes ancestors selecting the La Loche area for its vegetation and wildlife, and members living both on reserve and in La Loche itself. Its treaty reference differs from OpenBand’s existing source record, so an exact adhesion date is not repeated here. The community’s own history and the original treaty records require separate consideration when resolving that discrepancy. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/clearwater_river_dene_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Clearwater River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical names\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Big C; La Loche Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/clearwater_river_dene_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Clearwater River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dene \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/clearwater_river_dene_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Clearwater River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>La Loche area, Saskatchewan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/clearwater_river_dene_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Clearwater River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">The encyclopedia associates ancestors with Treaty 8 in 1899, while OpenBand currently lists Treaty 10. This brief leaves the treaty field unresolved instead of treating either entry as conclusive. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/clearwater_river_dene_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Clearwater River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "366": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Cote First Nation’s treaty-era history includes Chief Gabriel Cote’s signing of Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. His followers included Cree and Saulteaux people, and a reserve was surveyed in 1877. Subsequent land transactions around Kamsack repeatedly altered the reserve. Land was taken out of reserve status for a railway station and townsite in 1904, with further surrenders in the following years. Some land was later restored, including a southern strip returned in 1915 and additional acreage in 1963. This changing land base is an important part of the community’s historical experience and should be distinguished from its longer Indigenous history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cote_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cote First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cote_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cote First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cote_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cote First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Gabriel Cote \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cote_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cote First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cote_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cote First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "361": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Cowessess First Nation’s treaty history includes Chief Cowessess, whose name is also recorded as Ka-wezauce, signing Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. His followers included Saulteaux, Cree and Métis people. Families continued moving across their territory before some began farming near Maple Creek in 1878–79. A reserve was surveyed at Crooked Lake in 1880; while some members settled under O’Soup, Cowessess and others remained in the Cypress Hills until 1883. Education and mission institutions later shaped reserve life, including the opening of Cowessess Indian Residential School in 1898. These colonial-era milestones do not encompass the whole history of the community. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cowessess_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cowessess First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cowessess_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cowessess First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cowessess_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cowessess First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cowessess (Ka-wezauce) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cowessess_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cowessess First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1880, Crooked Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cowessess_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cowessess First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "350": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Cumberland House Cree Nation joined Treaty 5 through an adhesion in September 1876. Its reserve was surveyed in 1883, including Chief’s Island, where the chief, a councillor and other members lived. The chief sought additional land near Fort à la Corne because of its agricultural potential, but officials refused a reserve outside the treaty area. Members grew vegetables on Chief’s Island, while grain cultivation proved less successful. Hunting, fishing and trapping remained important to community life alongside later forestry and guiding work. The written account focuses on treaty and reserve decisions rather than defining the beginning of Cree presence in the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cumberland_house_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cumberland House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cumberland_house_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cumberland House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cumberland_house_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cumberland House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1883 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cumberland_house_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cumberland House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Chief’s Island \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/cumberland_house_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cumberland House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "389": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Day Star First Nation’s history includes hunting along the south branch of the Saskatchewan River before Chief Day Star, Kii-si-caw-ah-chuck, signed Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. A reserve was surveyed in 1876, with later boundary changes. Early farming included a jointly maintained community garden and a practice of sharing cattle so other families could establish herds. The arrival of the railway at Lipton in 1905 created a market for produce. Agricultural work later changed with mechanization, including a tractor purchase in 1946. These details document adaptation during the reserve period without reducing the community’s older history to a treaty date. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/day_star_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Day Star First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/day_star_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Day Star First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/day_star_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Day Star First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Day Star (Kii-si-caw-ah-chuck) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/day_star_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Day Star First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/day_star_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Day Star First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "352": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Hatchet Lake Denesuline’s treaty adhesion followed negotiations at Lac du Brochet in August 1907. Commissioner Thomas Borthwick met the Lac la Hache people on August 19; after choosing their chief and headmen, they signed Treaty 10 on August 22. The people’s hunting, fishing and trapping extended beyond a permanent settlement. The encyclopedia describes a shift toward year-round community life as regular school attendance increased around 1960. Their reserve on the eastern side of Wollaston Lake was surveyed in 1965 and set apart in 1970. The earlier name Axe Lake helps explain the English name Hatchet Lake, alongside the French Lac la Hache. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/hatchet_lake_denesuline_nation_lac_la_hache.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hatchet Lake Denesuline — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 10 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/hatchet_lake_denesuline_nation_lac_la_hache.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hatchet Lake Denesuline — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 22, 1907 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/hatchet_lake_denesuline_nation_lac_la_hache.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hatchet Lake Denesuline — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lac du Brochet \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/hatchet_lake_denesuline_nation_lac_la_hache.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hatchet Lake Denesuline — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve set apart\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 23, 1970 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/hatchet_lake_denesuline_nation_lac_la_hache.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hatchet Lake Denesuline — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "397": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Ministikwan Lake Cree Nation’s reserve history includes a long effort to obtain a separate land base. When the people entered Treaty 6 in 1876 without a chief recognized by officials, they were treated as part of Seekaskootch’s Onion Lake band. A 1907 petition sought their own reserve, leading to action in 1909 and establishment of the Island Lake Reserve in 1911. Officials did not follow the requested shoreline boundaries, which were intended to protect fish stocks. Hunting, fishing and trapping continued alongside gardening and forestry work. These administrative decisions describe reserve creation, rather than the beginning of the people’s connection to the lake. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/island_lake_band_ministikwan_indian_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ministikwan Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/island_lake_band_ministikwan_indian_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ministikwan Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty entry\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/island_lake_band_ministikwan_indian_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ministikwan Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 3, 1911 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/island_lake_band_ministikwan_indian_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ministikwan Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Island Lake Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/island_lake_band_ministikwan_indian_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ministikwan Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/onion-lake-cree-nation/\">Onion Lake Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Officials initially grouped the Ministikwan people with Seekaskootch’s Onion Lake band; Ministikwan later petitioned for a separate reserve. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/island_lake_band_ministikwan_indian_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ministikwan Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "370": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">James Smith Cree Nation’s written treaty history includes James Smith’s movement from St. Peter’s Reserve in Manitoba in 1875 and his signing of Treaty 6 on August 23, 1876. A reserve east of Prince Albert was surveyed in 1884. Later government actions brought people from other historical bands onto the reserve, including members of Chakastaypasin after its land was removed from reserve status in 1898 and Peter Chapman through an amalgamation in 1902. The encyclopedia discusses land grievances arising from these decisions. These historical band identities should be preserved in the account, rather than treating the community as having a single administrative origin. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/james_smith_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: James Smith Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/james_smith_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: James Smith Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 23, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/james_smith_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: James Smith Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>James Smith \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/james_smith_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: James Smith Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1884 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/james_smith_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: James Smith Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "362": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Kahkewistahaw First Nation’s history includes Cree and Saulteaux people who hunted in the Wood Mountain and Cypress Hills regions. Chief Kahkewistahaw signed Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. Families returned to the Qu’Appelle Valley for treaty payments before a reserve was surveyed in 1881. Agriculture developed despite difficult weather, but a large surrender in January 1907 removed much of the band’s most productive land and displaced farmers. A specific claim submitted in 1989 led to a settlement agreement signed on June 25, 2003. The claim and its resolution form part of the Nation’s continuing response to treaty-era land losses. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kahkewistahaw_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kahkewistahaw First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kahkewistahaw_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kahkewistahaw First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kahkewistahaw_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kahkewistahaw First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kahkewistahaw \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kahkewistahaw_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kahkewistahaw First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land claim agreement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>June 25, 2003 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kahkewistahaw_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kahkewistahaw First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "393": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Kawacatoose First Nation’s treaty-era history includes Chief Kawacatoose signing Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. Reserve land in the Big Touchwood Hills followed two years later. Colonial records often called the reserve Poor Man, although the encyclopedia translates Kawacatoose more closely as Lean Man or Skinny Man. After the chief’s death, his son Tawequasequape succeeded him. Members combined agriculture with sales of wood, hay and fence materials; a community farm began in 1949. In 1981, administration of the day school passed from Indian Affairs to the Nation. These events document changes in land, livelihoods and educational control during the reserve period. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kawacatoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kawacatoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kawacatoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kawacatoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kawacatoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kawacatoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kawacatoose \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kawacatoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kawacatoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Big Touchwood Hills \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kawacatoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kawacatoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "367": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Keeseekoose First Nation’s treaty history includes Chief Keeseekoose’s signing of Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. The people initially settled on the Swan River Reserve in Manitoba, but flooding led to relocation to the Pelly and Kamsack area in Saskatchewan. Their reserve lies between Cote and The Key. The university account also records the later placement of the band-controlled St. Phillip’s School on the former residential-school grounds. The relocation is an important part of the Nation’s reserve history; neither it nor the treaty signing represents the beginning of the people’s Indigenous history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/keeseekoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/keeseekoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/keeseekoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Keeseekoose \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/keeseekoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier reserve location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Swan River, Manitoba \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/keeseekoose_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoose First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "353": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Lac La Ronge Indian Band’s recorded history includes the James Roberts Wood Cree Band’s adhesion to Treaty 6 at Montreal Lake on February 11, 1889. Families hunted, fished and trapped around Lac la Ronge, maintaining lakeside camps and gardens. Communities developed at Lac la Ronge and Stanley Mission. Indian Affairs treated them as the James Roberts and Amos Charles bands in 1910, with separate annuity lists and funds but without formally dividing reserve land. The two bands were amalgamated on March 27, 1950. Their earlier land use and community identities extended beyond the boundaries and administrative names found in these records. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lac_la_ronge_indian_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lac La Ronge Indian Band — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lac_la_ronge_indian_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lac La Ronge Indian Band — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>February 11, 1889 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lac_la_ronge_indian_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lac La Ronge Indian Band — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Montreal Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lac_la_ronge_indian_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lac La Ronge Indian Band — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band amalgamation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>March 27, 1950 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lac_la_ronge_indian_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lac La Ronge Indian Band — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/montreal-lake-cree-nation/\">Montreal Lake Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Montreal Lake and Lac La Ronge shared the Little Red River agricultural reserve before its division in 1948. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lac_la_ronge_indian_band.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lac La Ronge Indian Band — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "379": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Little Black Bear First Nation’s history includes Cree and Assiniboine families who hunted and camped in the Cypress Hills during the nineteenth century. Chief Little Black Bear, recorded as Kees kee hew mus-coo muskwa, signed Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. The band later settled on reserve land surveyed in the File Hills in 1880. Farming became an important part of reserve life, shaped by weather, available equipment and government policy. The treaty and reserve survey document changes in the community’s relationship with the Crown; they do not mark the beginning of its people or their connections to the Plains. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_black_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Black Bear First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_black_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Black Bear First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_black_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Black Bear First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Little Black Bear \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_black_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Black Bear First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1880, File Hills \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_black_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Black Bear First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "340": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Little Pine First Nation’s ancestors were associated with the Cypress Hills before Chief Minahikosis, or Little Pine, joined Treaty 6 near Fort Walsh in 1879. The people were later moved to the Battle River area. Requests for adjoining reserves with Lucky Man, Poundmaker and Big Bear were refused. Little Pine died in 1885, and his people were dispersed during that period. A reserve for remaining Little Pine and Lucky Man members was surveyed in 1887. A treaty land entitlement settlement on May 29, 1997 addressed an inadequate land allocation and enabled further land purchases. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_pine_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Pine First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_pine_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Pine First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1879 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_pine_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Pine First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Minahikosis (Little Pine) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_pine_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Pine First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>May 29, 1997 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_pine_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Pine First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/lucky-man-cree-nation/\">Lucky Man Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Little Pine and Lucky Man members shared a reserve after dispersal in 1885; both sought adjoining reserves in the earlier treaty period. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/little_pine_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Pine First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "341": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Lucky Man Cree Nation’s treaty-era history includes Papaway, or Lucky Man, who was a headman in Big Bear’s band when he adhered to Treaty 6 at Fort Walsh in 1879. The people moved toward Battleford in 1883. The following year, a request for a reserve adjoining Poundmaker, Little Pine and Big Bear was refused. Many Lucky Man and Little Pine members were dispersed in 1885; some settled together on Little Pine’s reserve. Research begun in 1972 supported a land claim that led to selection of land in the Thickwood Hills in 1989. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lucky_man_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lucky Man Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lucky_man_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lucky Man Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1879, Fort Walsh \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lucky_man_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lucky Man Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Papaway (Lucky Man) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lucky_man_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lucky Man Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land selection\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1989, Thickwood Hills \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lucky_man_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lucky Man Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/little-pine-first-nation/\">Little Pine First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Some Lucky Man members lived on Little Pine’s reserve following the dispersal of both communities in 1885. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/lucky_man_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lucky Man Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "396": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Makwa Sahgaiehcan First Nation’s history is connected to Makwa Lake and the Loon Lake district. The university account records the Makwa Lake Cree Band joining Treaty 6 on September 9, 1876. Members continued their established way of life for decades before obtaining the Makwa Lake reserves in 1916. Cattle raising and farming became important after the reserve land base was secured. This distinction matters: the 1916 reserve decision was an administrative milestone, rather than the origin of the Cree community. The historical name Loon Lake also appears in accounts of the band. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/makwa_sahgaiehcan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Makwa Sahgaiehcan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/makwa_sahgaiehcan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Makwa Sahgaiehcan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 9, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/makwa_sahgaiehcan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Makwa Sahgaiehcan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserves received\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1916 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/makwa_sahgaiehcan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Makwa Sahgaiehcan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Makwa Lake Cree Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/makwa_sahgaiehcan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Makwa Sahgaiehcan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "374": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Mistawasis Nehiyawak’s treaty history includes Chief Mistawasis, leader of the Cree House People, signing Treaty 6 on August 23, 1876. A reserve was surveyed at Snake Plains in 1878, where wood, water, hay and agricultural land supported the community. The Green Lake Trail crossed the reserve and connected it with markets and freighting work. A school was established in 1878. The university account also records a 2001 settlement concerning land surrendered in 1911, 1917 and 1919. These treaty, transport and land-claim events form one part of a history rooted in Cree kinship and land use. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mistawasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mistawasis Nehiyawak — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mistawasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mistawasis Nehiyawak — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 23, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mistawasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mistawasis Nehiyawak — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Mistawasis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mistawasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mistawasis Nehiyawak — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1878, Snake Plains \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mistawasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mistawasis Nehiyawak — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/ahtahkakoop-cree-nation/\">Ahtahkakoop Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Ahtahkakoop requested reserve land adjoining Mistawasis along the Green Lake Trail. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mistawasis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mistawasis Nehiyawak — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "354": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Montreal Lake Cree Nation’s ancestors moved from the Grand Rapids region near Lake Winnipeg to Montreal Lake during the nineteenth century. Under Chief William Charles, the band joined Treaty 6 on February 11, 1889. A reserve was surveyed at the south end of Montreal Lake in 1890. The Little Red River Reserve was later surveyed for shared agricultural use with Lac La Ronge and divided in 1948. A Hudson’s Bay Company post established in 1891 became a regional freighting depot. Road and air transport later changed employment and helped develop tourism, alongside forestry and other activities. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/montreal_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montreal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/montreal_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montreal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>February 11, 1889 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/montreal_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montreal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>William Charles \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/montreal_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montreal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1890 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/montreal_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montreal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/lac-la-ronge-indian-band/\">Lac La Ronge Indian Band\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The two bands shared the Little Red River agricultural reserve before its division in 1948. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/montreal_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montreal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "342": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Moosomin First Nation’s treaty record differs from the original Treaty 6 negotiations in 1876. Yellow Sky’s band was absent from those talks; his headman Moosomin signed an adhesion in spring 1881. A reserve near Battleford was surveyed that year, and Moosomin became chief in 1884. Railway construction through the reserve in 1903 increased employment and market access but was followed by pressure for its land. A surrender in 1909 led to relocation near Cochin. The Nation challenged the surrender in a claim submitted in 1986, and a settlement was ratified in 2003. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/moosomin_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Moosomin First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/moosomin_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Moosomin First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Spring 1881 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/moosomin_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Moosomin First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Yellow Sky; Moosomin \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/moosomin_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Moosomin First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Settlement ratified\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2003 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/moosomin_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Moosomin First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "343": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Mosquito, Grizzly Bear’s Head, Lean Man brings together historical bands with distinct treaty-era paths. Misketo signed a Treaty 6 adhesion on August 29, 1878, succeeding Little Chief; the Mosquito people settled in the Eagle Hills in 1879–80. Grizzly Bear’s Head had joined Treaty 4 under Tepee Hoska in 1877 before moving toward Battleford after his death. Lean Man also entered Treaty 4 and later settled in the Treaty 6 area. The encyclopedia records a merger in 1951. This history explains why a single modern treaty label cannot fully describe the component bands’ experiences. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mosquito_grizzly_bears_head_lean_man_first_nations.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosquito, Grizzly Bear&#x27;s Head, Lean Man — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty history\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaties 4 and 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mosquito_grizzly_bears_head_lean_man_first_nations.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosquito, Grizzly Bear&#x27;s Head, Lean Man — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Mosquito adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 29, 1878 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mosquito_grizzly_bears_head_lean_man_first_nations.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosquito, Grizzly Bear&#x27;s Head, Lean Man — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Misketo; Tepee Hoska; Lean Man \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mosquito_grizzly_bears_head_lean_man_first_nations.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosquito, Grizzly Bear&#x27;s Head, Lean Man — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Eagle Hills and Battleford district \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/mosquito_grizzly_bears_head_lean_man_first_nations.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosquito, Grizzly Bear&#x27;s Head, Lean Man — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "381": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Muscowpetung First Nation’s treaty history includes an Ojibway and Cree band joining Treaty 4 under Chief Muscowpetung on September 8, 1875. The people continued moving through the Cypress Hills region before settling on a reserve surveyed beside Pasqua in 1881. Agriculture developed after settlement. In 1882, the chief requested an extension westward along the Qu’Appelle River to gain better access to wood; officials changed other boundaries as part of that adjustment. These decisions illustrate how access to resources shaped the reserve’s development, alongside the people’s older relationships with the surrounding territory. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muscowpetung_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muscowpetung First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muscowpetung_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muscowpetung First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 8, 1875 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muscowpetung_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muscowpetung First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Muscowpetung \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muscowpetung_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muscowpetung First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1881 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muscowpetung_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muscowpetung First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/pasqua-first-nation/\">Pasqua First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Muscowpetung’s reserve was surveyed adjoining Pasqua’s reserve in 1881. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muscowpetung_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muscowpetung First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "375": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Muskeg Lake Cree Nation joined Treaty 6 on August 23, 1876 under Chief Kitowehow, whose name also appears as Keetoowahan or Keetoowahaw. Members continued established livelihoods after treaty, selling furs and planting small potato plots. A reserve adjoining Mistawasis was surveyed in 1881. Its hay meadows, farmland, water and timber supported later farming and other work. The university account records employment with nearby settlers during the late 1880s and later commercial development. The treaty and survey belong to the reserve-era written record; they do not define the full extent of the Nation’s Cree history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskeg_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskeg Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskeg_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskeg Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 23, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskeg_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskeg Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kitowehow \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskeg_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskeg Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1881 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskeg_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskeg Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/mistawasis-nehiyawak/\">Mistawasis Nehiyawak\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Muskeg Lake’s original surveyed reserve adjoined Mistawasis on its southern boundary. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskeg_lake_cree_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskeg Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "371": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Muskoday First Nation’s history includes ancestors from St. Peter’s Reserve near Selkirk, Manitoba. John Smith, Mis-ti-koo-na-pac, and his followers signed Treaty 6 at Fort Carlton on August 23, 1876. Their reserve southeast of Prince Albert was surveyed the following month. Many families settled in river lots, while later surveys divided other land into sections. Farming and cattle raising were important in the early reserve period, and a band farm was established in 1963. In 1998, Muskoday adopted its own land code, an important change in the administration of leasing and land development. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskoday_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskoday First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskoday_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskoday First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 23, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskoday_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskoday First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Carlton \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskoday_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskoday First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>John Smith (Mis-ti-koo-na-pac) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskoday_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskoday First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "392": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Muskowekwan First Nation’s ancestors lived around the Upper Qu’Appelle Lakes before joining Treaty 4 under Chief Ka-nee-na-wup on September 15, 1874. His son Muscowequan, also called Hard Quill, later became chief. A reserve surveyed in 1883 included an existing farming settlement near Touchwood Post. Agriculture and educational institutions shaped subsequent reserve life. The university account records ratification of a treaty land entitlement claim in 1993, enabling the Nation to expand its land holdings. These recorded changes concern treaty and reserve administration within a longer Ojibway history of the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskowekwan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskowekwan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskowekwan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskowekwan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskowekwan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskowekwan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ka-nee-na-wup; Muscowequan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskowekwan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskowekwan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Entitlement ratified\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1993 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/muskowekwan_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Muskowekwan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "380": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Nekaneet Cree First Nation’s history is closely connected to remaining in the Cypress Hills when other communities were forced away in 1882. The university account associates the band’s Treaty 4 relationship with Foremost Man, also called Front Man, in 1874. Members were denied reserve land for decades before a small reserve near Maple Creek was established on August 2, 1913. Further land was added in 1958. Access to treaty benefits and schooling was also delayed. Later claims sought recognition of outstanding obligations. This history centres persistence in the people’s homeland, rather than treating reserve creation as the community’s beginning. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/nekaneet_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nekaneet Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/nekaneet_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nekaneet Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Foremost Man (Front Man) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/nekaneet_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nekaneet Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 2, 1913 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/nekaneet_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nekaneet Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cypress Hills \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/nekaneet_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nekaneet Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "408": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Ocean Man First Nation’s treaty-era history includes Chief Kitchi-Kah-Me-Win’s adhesion to Treaty 4 on September 9, 1875. A reserve beside Pheasant Rump in the Moose Mountains was surveyed in 1882. The people developed farms, but surrendered their reserve and were amalgamated with White Bear in 1901 after sustained pressure. Descendants later pursued a land claim and the restoration of their separate community. The band was re-established in 1988 and its reserve in 1992. The restored Nation carries an earlier history that continued through displacement; its modern administrative re-establishment was not the beginning of its people. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ocean_man_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ocean Man First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ocean_man_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ocean Man First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 9, 1875 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ocean_man_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ocean Man First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kitchi-Kah-Me-Win (Ocean Man) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ocean_man_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ocean Man First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band re-established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1988 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ocean_man_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ocean Man First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/white-bear-first-nations/\">White Bear First Nations\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Ocean Man was amalgamated with White Bear in 1901 before its separate band status was restored in 1988. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ocean_man_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ocean Man First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/pheasant-rump-nakota-nation/\">Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The original Ocean Man and Pheasant Rump reserves were adjacent in the Moose Mountains. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ocean_man_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ocean Man First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "363": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Ochapowace First Nation’s written history includes the distinct Kakisheway and Chacachas bands. Kakisheway, also known as Loud Voice, signed Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. His reserve was surveyed near Round Lake and the Qu’Appelle River in 1876. Subsequent government decisions placed the two bands on shared reserve land despite objections from members who had been away hunting. Ochapowace succeeded his father Kakisheway in 1884 as chief of the amalgamated bands. Their separate identities and the effects of that amalgamation are important to the community’s history; later land-claim developments require their own contemporary sources. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ochapowace_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ochapowace First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ochapowace_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ochapowace First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ochapowace_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ochapowace First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kakisheway; Chacachas; Ochapowace \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ochapowace_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ochapowace First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Original reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/ochapowace_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ochapowace First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "382": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Okanese First Nation’s treaty record includes Chief Okanis, or Okanese, joining Treaty 4 on September 9, 1875 and settling in the File Hills. After his death, Moostooacoop served as headman until 1895. Members continued established ways of living while also developing gardens, crop farming and cattle herds as resources became scarce. Railway access at Balcarres in the early twentieth century created markets and employment. The community’s history also includes the File Hills Residential School, established in 1879 and closed in 1950. A treaty land entitlement framework agreement followed in December 1999. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/okanese_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Okanese First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/okanese_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Okanese First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 9, 1875 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/okanese_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Okanese First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Okanis (Okanese) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/okanese_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Okanese First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Entitlement framework\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>December 1999 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/okanese_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Okanese First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "373": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">One Arrow First Nation’s treaty record includes entry into Treaty 6 on September 6, 1878. The people settled on their reserve near the South Saskatchewan River in autumn 1880. The university account describes the site as suitable for agriculture and records the leadership of headman Crowskin in 1882, when the chief was elderly. The reserve period brought new forms of farming and settlement alongside older Cree relationships with the river country. The treaty date and reserve settlement are documented milestones, rather than a complete account of the people’s history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/one_arrow_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: One Arrow First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/one_arrow_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: One Arrow First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 6, 1878 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/one_arrow_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: One Arrow First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1880 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/one_arrow_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: One Arrow First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical headman\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Crowskin \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/one_arrow_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: One Arrow First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "344": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Onion Lake Cree Nation’s history brings together the Seekaskootch and Makaoo bands. Seekaskootch’s people signed Treaty 6 at Fort Pitt on September 9, 1876, while the Makaoo band joined through an adhesion in 1878. Adjacent reserves followed in 1879. After 1885, federal officials classified both communities as rebellious and withheld recognition of a chief. In 1914, recognition was made conditional on amalgamation as the Onion Lake Band. Their reserve crosses the present Saskatchewan–Alberta boundary. These administrative events should be distinguished from the Cree communities’ earlier histories and from individual responsibility for events in 1885. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/onion_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Onion Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/onion_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Onion Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Seekaskootch signing\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 9, 1876, Fort Pitt \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/onion_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Onion Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Makaoo adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1878 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/onion_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Onion Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band amalgamation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1914 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/onion_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Onion Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "383": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Pasqua First Nation’s treaty history includes Chief Joseph Pasqua signing Treaty 4 in September 1874. He selected land beside Pasqua Lake so his people could rely on fish and waterfowl when other game was scarce. Pasqua challenged inadequate treaty provisions and sought help for people facing hunger. After his death in 1889, the community petitioned for recognition of another chief; his son Ben was elected in 1911. A land surrender in 1906 later became the subject of a claim. The account records a diverse community and the effects of restrictive reserve administration. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pasqua_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pasqua First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pasqua_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pasqua First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty signing\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pasqua_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pasqua First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Joseph Pasqua \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pasqua_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pasqua First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Pasqua Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pasqua_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pasqua First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "384": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Peepeekisis Cree Nation’s history includes Chief Can-ah-ha-cha-pew’s signing of Treaty 4 on September 21, 1874. His son Peepeekisis later became chief, and the people moved from the Cypress Hills to the File Hills in 1884. Beginning in 1898, Indian Agent W. M. Graham placed graduates from industrial schools on the reserve through the File Hills Colony scheme. Original members were displaced from homes and communal land. Complaints eventually led to a specific claim in 1986 and an Indian Claims Commission finding in 2004. This account concerns the historical scheme and does not describe the claim’s current status. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peepeekisis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peepeekisis Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peepeekisis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peepeekisis Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 21, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peepeekisis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peepeekisis Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Can-ah-ha-cha-pew; Peepeekisis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peepeekisis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peepeekisis Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>File Hills Colony began\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1898 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peepeekisis_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peepeekisis Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "405": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Pelican Lake First Nation’s history is associated with Cree families living near Pelican Lake and later Chitek Lake. The university account records entry into Treaty 6 in 1889 and continued hunting and fishing. Early department records associated the people with Kenemotayoo’s Big River band even while they lived separately. Reserve surveys and leadership arrangements developed over subsequent years. Louis Chamakee is recorded as the first chief. Farming supplemented other livelihoods, but trapping and wood sales remained important. This history distinguishes the community’s own settlement pattern from the administrative grouping used by officials. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pelican_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pelican Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pelican_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pelican Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty entry\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1889 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pelican_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pelican Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Louis (Lewis) Chamakee \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pelican_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pelican Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Pelican and Chitek lakes \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pelican_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pelican Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/big-river-first-nation/\">Big River First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Early twentieth-century records associated Pelican Lake with Kenemotayoo’s Big River band and shared reserve ownership, despite separate residence. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pelican_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pelican Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "355": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Peter Ballantyne Cree Nation’s documented treaty history is connected to the Lac La Ronge people’s adhesion to Treaty 6 on February 11, 1889. The university account places its ancestors within that earlier administrative grouping. The Nation’s land base encompasses several northern communities, including Pelican Narrows and Southend near Reindeer Lake, rather than a single settlement. Fishing, trapping and forestry feature prominently in the historical account of livelihoods. The treaty record describes a relationship with the Crown; it should not be taken as the beginning of Cree residence or as a complete explanation of the communities’ distinct histories. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peter_ballantyne_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peter Ballantyne Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peter_ballantyne_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peter Ballantyne Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier treaty grouping\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lac La Ronge Indian Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peter_ballantyne_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peter Ballantyne Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical livelihoods\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fishing, trapping and forestry \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peter_ballantyne_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peter Ballantyne Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/lac-la-ronge-indian-band/\">Lac La Ronge Indian Band\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The university account connects Peter Ballantyne’s ancestors with the Lac La Ronge band’s 1889 Treaty 6 adhesion. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/peter_ballantyne_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peter Ballantyne Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "409": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation’s reserve history includes a Treaty 4 adhesion in 1876 and the granting of a reserve in 1881. Members farmed near Kisbey while also earning income from freighting, woodcutting and skilled work. In 1901, the band was amalgamated with White Bear after officials pursued surrender of its reserve. Descendants later sought restoration of their community and land base. A settlement agreement was signed in 1986, separate band recognition followed in 1990, and purchased land received reserve status in 1992. That restoration continued an older Nakota community history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pheasants_rump_nakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pheasants_rump_nakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pheasants_rump_nakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band recognition restored\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1990 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pheasants_rump_nakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve status restored\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1992 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pheasants_rump_nakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/white-bear-first-nations/\">White Bear First Nations\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Pheasant Rump was amalgamated with White Bear in 1901 before separate recognition was restored in 1990. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/pheasants_rump_nakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "385": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Piapot Cree First Nation’s history includes Chief Piapot, also recorded as Payepot, joining Treaty 4 on September 9, 1875. He initially sought a reserve in the Cypress Hills. After pressure to relocate, the people settled beside Carry the Kettle in 1883, but a severe winter and many deaths led Piapot to seek another location. In September 1884, the band selected land northeast of Regina. Piapot’s resistance to official interference in ceremonies forms an important part of his leadership history. The successive reserve locations reflect displacement and negotiation, rather than the full extent of the people’s homeland. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/piapot_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piapot First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/piapot_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piapot First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 9, 1875 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/piapot_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piapot First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Piapot (Payepot) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/piapot_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piapot First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>New reserve selection\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 1884 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/piapot_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piapot First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/carry-the-kettle-nakoda-nation/\">Carry the Kettle Nakoda Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Piapot’s people briefly settled on a reserve bordering Carry the Kettle in 1883 before moving to another location. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/piapot_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piapot First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "345": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Poundmaker Cree Nation’s history includes the leadership of Pitikwahanapiwiyin, or Poundmaker, and the development of a separate band after his earlier association with Red Pheasant’s people. He participated in the Treaty 6 era and selected reserve land northwest of Battleford in 1881. Following his death, the band went without a recognized chief until 1920. His remains were returned to the reserve and buried at Cut Knife Hill in 1967. The university account also records later treaty land entitlement acquisitions. An inconsistent exact signing date in that entry is deliberately omitted here. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/poundmaker_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poundmaker Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/poundmaker_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poundmaker Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Pitikwahanapiwiyin (Poundmaker) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/poundmaker_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poundmaker Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve selection\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1881 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/poundmaker_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poundmaker Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reburial at Cut Knife Hill\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1967 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/poundmaker_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poundmaker Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/red-pheasant-cree-nation/\">Red Pheasant Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Poundmaker was formerly associated with Red Pheasant’s band before leading a separate community. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/poundmaker_cree_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poundmaker Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "356": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Red Earth Cree Nation’s history is rooted in the Carrot River district and the western Saskatchewan River delta. Late nineteenth-century settlements included families associated with Kiseyinis, or Little Old Man, and Cecim. The university account records their Treaty 5 adhesion on September 7, 1876, with reserves surveyed at Red Earth in 1884 and Carrot River in 1894. Hunting, fishing, gardens and livestock sustained families. Drought and declining fur resources in the 1930s, together with encroachment on hunting grounds, increased reliance on agriculture and paid work. These events concern adaptation within a much older Cree landscape. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_earth_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Earth Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty in historical account\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_earth_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Earth Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 7, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_earth_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Earth Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kiseyinis; Cecim \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_earth_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Earth Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve surveys\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1884 and 1894 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_earth_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Earth Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">The university history identifies Treaty 5, whereas the existing profile dataset lists Treaty 6. The historical account is cited directly here; the existing profile classification has not been changed. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_earth_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Earth Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "346": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Red Pheasant Cree Nation’s ancestors hunted, fished and traded along the Battle River before treaty. Chief Wuttunee, or Porcupine, opposed Treaty 6 and asked his brother Red Pheasant to sign on his behalf on September 9, 1876. Officials subsequently recognized Red Pheasant as chief. The people settled on their Eagle Hills reserve in 1878, where farmland and wooded areas supported their livelihoods. A day school opened in 1880 and an Anglican church followed in 1885. Later treaty land entitlement work expanded the reserve land base. The two brothers’ different roles are important to understanding the signing record. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_pheasant_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Pheasant Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_pheasant_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Pheasant Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 9, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_pheasant_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Pheasant Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Wuttunee; Red Pheasant \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_pheasant_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Pheasant Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1878 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/red_pheasant_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Pheasant Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "364": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sakimay First Nations’ written treaty history includes Chief Sakimay, whose name also appears as Sah-ka-ma or Sah-kee-may, signing Treaty 4 on September 15, 1874. The reserve formed part of the western Crooked Lake reserve district. After the chief’s death, Yellow Calf acted as a spokesman in dealings with the Indian Agent during the early 1880s. The community’s land base includes the Sakimay, Shesheep, Little Bone and Minoahcuk reserves. Agriculture and later tourism appear in the historical account. The treaty record and reserve names capture only part of the people’s continuing history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sakimay_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sakimay First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sakimay_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sakimay First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 15, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sakimay_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sakimay First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Sakimay (Sah-kee-may) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sakimay_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sakimay First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical district\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Crooked Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sakimay_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sakimay First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "347": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Saulteaux First Nation’s history includes a long-standing Treaty 6 land entitlement grievance and its eventual settlement. The university account records ratification of the Nation’s claim on April 7, 1993, enabling the purchase of additional agricultural, commercial, residential and other land in Saskatchewan. The community is described as Ojibway-speaking, with its main reserve settlement north of North Battleford. Its land claim concerned a treaty land debt, rather than the beginning of the Nation’s relationship with the land. The entry’s exact treaty date is not repeated here because it requires corroboration against the original adhesion records. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/saulteaux_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saulteaux First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/saulteaux_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saulteaux First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Entitlement ratified\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>April 7, 1993 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/saulteaux_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saulteaux First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ojibway \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/saulteaux_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saulteaux First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>North Battleford district \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/saulteaux_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saulteaux First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "357": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Shoal Lake Cree Nation’s history is rooted in the western Saskatchewan River delta, where Swampy Cree families lived during the nineteenth century. The university account identifies Okakeek, Osawask and Kis-moswakapaw among the community’s early recorded leaders and records a Treaty 5 adhesion in 1876. A reserve was surveyed in 1882. Gardening, livestock, hunting and fishing supported families, while later agricultural settlement nearby created markets and employment. Construction of an all-weather road in 1960–61 changed access to work and training. The village is also known as Pakwaw Lake. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/shoal_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shoal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty in historical account\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/shoal_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shoal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/shoal_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shoal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1882 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/shoal_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shoal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Pakwaw Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/shoal_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shoal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">The university history identifies Treaty 5; the existing OpenBand profile dataset lists Treaty 6. This historical section preserves the source distinction without changing the other profile data. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/shoal_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shoal Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "386": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Standing Buffalo Dakota Nation’s history includes Tatankanaje becoming hereditary leader after his father’s death in 1871. The community received permission to farm north of the Qu’Appelle lakes in 1877, and a reserve was established there in 1881. Members developed farms and earned wages, but the small land allocation restricted growth. Repeated requests for more land included a journey to Ottawa by Standing Buffalo and his son Julius in 1920. Additional reserve acreage was not granted until 1956. These events describe a Dakota reserve history that should not be reduced to the treaty labels used elsewhere in the directory. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/standing_buffalo_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Standing Buffalo Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Tatankanaje (Standing Buffalo) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/standing_buffalo_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Standing Buffalo Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1881 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/standing_buffalo_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Standing Buffalo Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land petition to Ottawa\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1920 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/standing_buffalo_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Standing Buffalo Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Additional acreage\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1956 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/standing_buffalo_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Standing Buffalo Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "387": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Star Blanket Cree Nation’s treaty history includes Chief Wah-pi-moos-toosis, or White Calf, joining Treaty 4 in 1874. His Calling River Cree people used territory extending through the File Hills, Wood Mountain and Cypress Hills. A reserve near Crooked Lake was surveyed in 1876, but the band did not settle there. Instead, it received land in the File Hills in 1880. Later land-claim work included transfer of the Lebret student residence in 1983 and Crown lots in Fort Qu’Appelle in 1994. These changes form part of the Nation’s reserve and land-recovery history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/starblanket_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Star Blanket Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/starblanket_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Star Blanket Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty entry\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/starblanket_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Star Blanket Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Wah-pi-moos-toosis (White Calf) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/starblanket_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Star Blanket Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>File Hills reserve\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1880 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/starblanket_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Star Blanket Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "360": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sturgeon Lake First Nation’s treaty-era record includes Chief Ah-yah-tus-kum-ik-im-am, also known as William Twatt, and four headmen signing Treaty 6 near Fort Carlton on August 23, 1876. A reserve at Sturgeon Lake followed in 1878. The community was long called the William Twatt Band before adopting the Sturgeon Lake name in 1963. Members combined farming and cattle raising with trapping, woodcutting and wage work. A grievance concerning timber revenue lost from 1906 was settled in 2001. This account concerns the Saskatchewan Nation and should not be confused with the separate Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation in Alberta. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sturgeon_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sturgeon_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 23, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sturgeon_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>William Twatt \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sturgeon_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Name change\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1963 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sturgeon_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "348": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sweetgrass First Nation’s history includes Chief Weekaskookwasayin, or Sweet Grass, signing Treaty 6 with the Fort Pitt communities on September 9, 1876. Following his death, his son Apseenes, or Young Sweet Grass, succeeded him. Members later joined people led by Wah-wee-kah-oo-tah-mah-hote, another Treaty 6 signatory. A reserve west of Battleford was surveyed in 1884 for the combined community. Gardens, livestock and sales of hay and wood supported families during the reserve period. The history involves changing leadership and the coming together of groups, rather than a single founding event. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sweet_grass_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sweetgrass First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sweet_grass_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sweetgrass First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 9, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sweet_grass_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sweetgrass First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Pitt \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sweet_grass_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sweetgrass First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Sweet Grass; Young Sweet Grass \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/sweet_grass_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sweetgrass First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "368": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">The Key First Nation’s people lived along the Shoal River in Manitoba before Chief Ow-tah-pee-ka-kaw, or The Key, signed a Treaty 4 adhesion on September 24, 1875. The band included families with Saulteaux, Cree and fur-trade connections. An initial reserve survey in 1878 was followed by relocation nearer Fort Pelly after flooding made the first site unsuitable. Skilled trades and agriculture helped families rebuild. Land surrenders in 1905 and 1909 reduced opportunities for agricultural expansion. The university account also records Gwen O’Soup’s election as chief in 1954, an important leadership milestone. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/the_key_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: The Key First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/the_key_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: The Key First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 24, 1875 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/the_key_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: The Key First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ow-tah-pee-ka-kaw (The Key) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/the_key_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: The Key First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Shoal River, Manitoba \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/the_key_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: The Key First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "358": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Wahpeton Dakota Nation’s ancestors used lands on both sides of the present Canada–United States border before settling near Prince Albert under Chief Hupa-yaktao in 1878. The community did not sign a numbered treaty. An Order in Council reserved land for it in 1894, with further acreage added in 1917 and 1930. Earlier Dakota encampments gradually combined their memberships. Farming later became important, although a limited land base constrained commercial development. Reserve creation was an administrative recognition within a much older Dakota history, rather than the beginning of the people’s existence or relationship with the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wahpeton_dakota_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wahpeton Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Non-signatory to a numbered treaty \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wahpeton_dakota_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wahpeton Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Hupa-yaktao \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wahpeton_dakota_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wahpeton Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve land set aside\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1894 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wahpeton_dakota_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wahpeton Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Additional land\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1917 and 1930 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wahpeton_dakota_reserve.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wahpeton Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "402": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Waterhen Lake First Nation’s recorded treaty history includes an adhesion to Treaty 6 under Chief Running Around in January 1913. A reserve was surveyed in 1916. The chief sought assurance that the people could continue their established way of life. Hunting and trapping areas extended around Lost Lake and toward Primrose Lake. Provincial fur-conservation regulations later changed access, followed by establishment of the Primrose Lake Air Weapons Range in 1954. Members joined other communities in seeking compensation for lost harvesting lands during the 1970s. These events describe restrictions on an older land-based Cree economy. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/waterhen_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waterhen Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/waterhen_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waterhen Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>January 1913 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/waterhen_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waterhen Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Running Around \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/waterhen_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waterhen Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1916 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/waterhen_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waterhen Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "365": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">White Bear First Nations’ history includes Chief Wahpemakwa, or White Bear, entering Treaty 4 in 1875 and accepting reserve land east of Moose Mountain in 1877. Members combined established livelihoods with agriculture. In 1901, officials amalgamated Ocean Man and Pheasant Rump onto White Bear’s reserve; a promised land extension followed only later and required payment by the band. Descendants of the affected communities eventually pursued claims concerning the surrendered reserves. Those separate band histories remain important when explaining White Bear’s past. The account should not imply that the amalgamation erased the identities of Ocean Man or Pheasant Rump. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/white_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: White Bear First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/white_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: White Bear First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty entry\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1875 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/white_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: White Bear First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Wahpemakwa (White Bear) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/white_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: White Bear First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve accepted\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/white_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: White Bear First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/ocean-man-first-nation/\">Ocean Man First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Ocean Man was amalgamated onto White Bear’s reserve in 1901 and later regained separate recognition. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/white_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: White Bear First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/pheasant-rump-nakota-nation/\">Pheasant Rump Nakota Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Pheasant Rump was also amalgamated onto White Bear in 1901 before its separate band status was restored. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/white_bear_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: White Bear First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "372": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Whitecap Dakota First Nation’s history includes Chief Wapahaska, or White Cap, and his people settling along the South Saskatchewan River. The community received a reserve in June 1881 without signing a numbered treaty. White Cap was tried after the 1885 Resistance and acquitted. Members returned to rebuild, developing cattle herds, farming and wage work. The university account describes later adaptation to changes in livestock markets. This is a history of a Dakota community with its own relationship to the Crown; a nearby treaty territory should not be mistaken for a treaty signed by Whitecap. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/whitecap_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitecap Dakota First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Non-signatory to a numbered treaty \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/whitecap_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitecap Dakota First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Wapahaska (White Cap) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/whitecap_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitecap Dakota First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>June 1881 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/whitecap_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitecap Dakota First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Moose Woods Sioux Reserve \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/whitecap_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitecap Dakota First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "407": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Witchekan Lake First Nation’s history includes land occupation and reserve surveys that preceded its formal Treaty 6 adhesion on November 21, 1950. The distinction matters because that relatively recent date does not mark the beginning of the Cree community. Agriculture, forestry, fishing and trapping feature in the university account of livelihoods. In 1997, the Nation acquired the Bapaume Community Pasture through a treaty land entitlement agreement. The acquisition expanded its land base and illustrates how treaty obligations continued to shape land recovery long after the original nineteenth-century Treaty 6 negotiations. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/witchekan_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Witchekan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/witchekan_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Witchekan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>November 21, 1950 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/witchekan_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Witchekan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land acquisition\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Bapaume Community Pasture, 1997 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/witchekan_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Witchekan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>North Battleford district \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/witchekan_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Witchekan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "388": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Wood Mountain First Nation’s Lakota history includes communities living and moving across the northern Plains before the present international boundary took its modern form. The university account describes settlement near Wood Mountain during the 1870s and the arrival of Sitting Bull’s followers. Some remained after he returned to the United States in 1881. They supported themselves through land-based livelihoods and wage work. A temporary reserve was created in 1910 and formally recognized in 1930. The people did not sign a numbered treaty with Canada; reserve recognition should not be presented as the beginning of Lakota presence in the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wood_mountain_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wood Mountain First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Non-signatory to a numbered treaty \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wood_mountain_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wood Mountain First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Temporary reserve\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>October 29, 1910 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wood_mountain_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wood Mountain First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve recognized\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 5, 1930 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wood_mountain_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wood Mountain First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Wood Mountain and northern Plains \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/wood_mountain_dakota_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wood Mountain First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "376": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Yellow Quill First Nation’s treaty history includes Chief Yellow Quill and headmen Kenistin and Ne-Pin-awa signing a Treaty 4 adhesion at Fort Pelly on August 24, 1876. Reserve surveys at Fishing and Nut lakes followed in 1881, and Yellow Quill selected Nut Lake. Hunting, fishing and trapping continued before diminishing wildlife resources increased reliance on farming during the 1930s. Grain production became important in the following decades. A treaty land entitlement framework agreement was ratified on September 23, 1992. The historical Nut Lake identity and the wider treaty-era band should be distinguished from present-day administrative boundaries. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/yellow_quill_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Yellow Quill First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/yellow_quill_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Yellow Quill First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 24, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/yellow_quill_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Yellow Quill First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Pelly \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/yellow_quill_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Yellow Quill First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Yellow Quill \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/yellow_quill_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Yellow Quill First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "377": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Kinistin Saulteaux Nation’s history includes movement from north of the Great Lakes into the Qu’Appelle Valley with Yellow Quill’s people. Their Treaty 4 adhesion was signed at Fort Pelly on August 24, 1876. Kinistin later led a separate community in the Barrier River district. The people moved north during the upheaval of 1885, then returned to seek reserve land. With assistance from Reginald Beatty, a reserve was established in 1900. The university account explains that Kinistino was a clerical rendering of Kinistin’s name. The community’s separate identity should be distinguished from that administrative spelling. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kinistino_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinistin Saulteaux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kinistino_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinistin Saulteaux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 24, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kinistino_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinistin Saulteaux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kinistin \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kinistino_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinistin Saulteaux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1900 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kinistino_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinistin Saulteaux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/yellow-quill-first-nation/\">Yellow Quill First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Kinistin’s people were historically associated with Yellow Quill before developing a separate community in the Barrier River district. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/kinistino_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinistin Saulteaux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "391": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">George Gordon First Nation’s history includes an enduring Treaty 4 land entitlement grievance. The Nation’s own account explains that some people were absent when the treaty census was taken, leaving a shortfall in the reserve land allocated under the treaty’s population-based formula. A settlement agreement was ratified on February 15, 2008 and became effective on August 11 that year. Canada and Saskatchewan provided compensation toward land acquisition, with settlement funds held under specified trust conditions. This land-recovery history concerns an outstanding treaty obligation, rather than the beginning of the Nation or its relationship with the territory. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ggfn.ca/treaty-land-entitlement-settlement/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: George Gordon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ggfn.ca/treaty-land-entitlement-settlement/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: George Gordon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Settlement ratified\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>February 15, 2008 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ggfn.ca/treaty-land-entitlement-settlement/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: George Gordon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Settlement effective\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 11, 2008 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ggfn.ca/treaty-land-entitlement-settlement/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: George Gordon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical issue\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Reserve land entitlement shortfall \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ggfn.ca/treaty-land-entitlement-settlement/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: George Gordon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "349": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Thunderchild First Nation’s treaty history includes an adhesion to Treaty 6 in August 1879, several years after the original negotiations. The Nation’s agricultural benefits claim information records that its people were initially required to live on Moosomin’s reserve. The treaty promised livestock, tools, seed and agricultural instruction, but the Nation’s account describes those obligations as inadequately fulfilled. Its later claim sought redress for that history. The distinction between the 1876 treaty negotiations and Thunderchild’s 1879 adhesion is important; neither date represents the beginning of the Cree community’s existence. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://thunderchild.ca/mrws/filedriver/Final2023-12-07_-_Thunderchild_-_Revenue_Account_Law_-_Information_Package_Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Thunderchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://thunderchild.ca/mrws/filedriver/Final2023-12-07_-_Thunderchild_-_Revenue_Account_Law_-_Information_Package_Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Thunderchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 1879 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://thunderchild.ca/mrws/filedriver/Final2023-12-07_-_Thunderchild_-_Revenue_Account_Law_-_Information_Package_Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Thunderchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical issue\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Unfulfilled agricultural benefits \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://thunderchild.ca/mrws/filedriver/Final2023-12-07_-_Thunderchild_-_Revenue_Account_Law_-_Information_Package_Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Thunderchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/moosomin-first-nation/\">Moosomin First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Thunderchild’s claim history records that members were required to live on Moosomin’s reserve after adhering to Treaty 6. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://thunderchild.ca/mrws/filedriver/Final2023-12-07_-_Thunderchild_-_Revenue_Account_Law_-_Information_Package_Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Thunderchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "400": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">English River Dene Nation’s history is associated with the Poplar House People and families who lived around Primeau, Knee, Cree and Dipper lakes before gathering at Patuanak. Meadow Lake Tribal Council identifies William Apesis as the chief at the 1906 signing of Treaty 10. The community’s earlier reserve name was Grassy Narrows. Fishing, hunting and gathering along the Churchill River remained important as settlement and educational institutions changed. An elementary school opened in 1971 and a high school was completed in 2000. These developments form part of the Nation’s history without defining the beginning of Dene occupation of the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.mltc.net/nations/erfn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: English River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 10 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.mltc.net/nations/erfn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: English River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1906 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.mltc.net/nations/erfn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: English River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>William Apesis (Apisis) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.mltc.net/nations/erfn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: English River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Grassy Narrows Reserve \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.mltc.net/nations/erfn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: English River Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "390": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Fishing Lake First Nation describes itself as an Anishnabe community with roots in the wider Saulteaux and Ojibway world. Its own history connects the people with movement into the Qu’Appelle Valley during the nineteenth century and with Chief Yellow Quill’s Treaty 4 adhesion. That earlier group included people who later formed distinct communities at Fishing Lake, Nut Lake and under Kinistin. The account emphasizes those relationships and the leadership involved in the westward and northward movements. It does not make the treaty the beginning of Anishnabe existence, and uncertain details about Yellow Quill’s birthplace are not treated as settled fact here. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fishinglakefirstnation.com/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fishing Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fishinglakefirstnation.com/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fishing Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Chief Yellow Quill \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fishinglakefirstnation.com/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fishing Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Cultural identity\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Anishnabe (Saulteaux/Ojibway) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fishinglakefirstnation.com/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fishing Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/yellow-quill-first-nation/\">Yellow Quill First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Fishing Lake’s own history links its treaty-era community with Yellow Quill’s people at Nut Lake. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fishinglakefirstnation.com/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fishing Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/kinistin-saulteaux-nation/\">Kinistin Saulteaux Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The Nation’s history identifies Kinistin as a leader within the wider Yellow Quill treaty-era group. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fishinglakefirstnation.com/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fishing Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "395": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Flying Dust First Nation’s own history connects Chief Kopahawakenum and the people around Green Lake with Treaty 6 and later settlement near Meadow Lake. Its account records the historical name Green Lake Indians in annuity lists and describes kinship with neighbouring Cree communities. Hunting, trapping and fishing supported families before the reserve pass system and declining resources encouraged greater reliance on agriculture. The Nation also records the withholding of annuities after officials wrongly classified its people as rebels in 1885. Railway land taken in 1932 later became the subject of a specific claim. Conflicting exact treaty dates on the page are omitted. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.flyingdust.net/history.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Flying Dust First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.flyingdust.net/history.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Flying Dust First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kopahawakenum (Flying Dust) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.flyingdust.net/history.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Flying Dust First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Green Lake Indians \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.flyingdust.net/history.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Flying Dust First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Railway land taking\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1932 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.flyingdust.net/history.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Flying Dust First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "403": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Birch Narrows Dene Nation’s community plan places its history in the Clear Lake region since time immemorial. Chief Raphael, whose name appears in several transliterations, signed Treaty 10 for the Clear Lake Band at Île-à-la-Crosse on August 28, 1906. Later records use the Peter Pond Lake Band name. Many members relocated in 1965, and Turnor Lake was recognized as a separate band in 1972. It adopted the Birch Narrows Dene Nation name in 1990. These administrative changes belong to a continuing Denesuline history; they should not be presented as the origin of the people or their homeland. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birch Narrows Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 10 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birch Narrows Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty signing\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 28, 1906 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birch Narrows Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier band name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Turnor Lake Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birch Narrows Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Birch Narrows name adopted\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1990 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birch Narrows Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/buffalo-river-dene-nation/\">Buffalo River Dene Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Birch Narrows and Buffalo River share the earlier Peter Pond Band history; the communities became separate bands in 1972. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birch Narrows Dene Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "351": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Fond du Lac Denesuline First Nation’s treaty-era history includes joining Treaty 8 in July 1899 as part of Maurice Piche’s band. Hunting, fishing and trapping continued across a broad northern territory. The encyclopedia describes efforts during the 1930s and 1940s to secure reserve land in northeastern Saskatchewan and the Northwest Territories. On January 11, 1960, Indian Affairs acknowledged an outstanding treaty land entitlement and allowed land selections. The community on Lake Athabasca therefore has a history that extends well beyond the timing of reserve recognition and the boundaries drawn in federal records. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://esask.uregina.ca/entry/fond_du_lac_denesuline_first_nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fond du Lac Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://esask.uregina.ca/entry/fond_du_lac_denesuline_first_nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fond du Lac Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://esask.uregina.ca/entry/fond_du_lac_denesuline_first_nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fond du Lac Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Maurice Piche \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://esask.uregina.ca/entry/fond_du_lac_denesuline_first_nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fond du Lac Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Entitlement acknowledged\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>January 11, 1960 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://esask.uregina.ca/entry/fond_du_lac_denesuline_first_nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fond du Lac Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/black-lake-denesuline-first-nation/\">Black Lake Denesuline First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Fond du Lac and Black Lake share the earlier Maurice Piche band history before their formal administrative division. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://esask.uregina.ca/entry/fond_du_lac_denesuline_first_nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fond du Lac Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "438": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Alexander First Nation, Kipohtakaw, describes a Cree history rooted in the forests, lakes and rivers of central Alberta long before European contact. The Nation takes its English name from Chief Catchistahwayskum, or Alexander, who signed a Treaty 6 adhesion in 1877. Its own account emphasizes continuity through Cree language, ceremonies and spiritual teachings, rather than treating the treaty as a founding date. Land and water stewardship remain part of that relationship with the territory. The history of reserve administration is therefore one chapter within a much longer history of the people around present-day Morinville. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alexanderfn.com/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alexanderfn.com/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alexanderfn.com/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Catchistahwayskum (Alexander) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alexanderfn.com/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kipohtakaw \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alexanderfn.com/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "437": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Alexis Nakota Sioux Nation’s own history centres on Mitaushin Aranazhi, Stands On Top, whose treaty-government name was recorded as Chief Alexis Kees-kee-chee-chi. He signed the Treaty 6 adhesion on August 21, 1877. The Nation recounts the ceremony, songs and teachings associated with the people’s journey to Fort Edmonton, preserving an oral account alongside the written record. Although Indian Affairs recognized a limited period of formal office, the community continued to recognize his leadership until his death in 1913. This distinction illustrates why government appointment records alone cannot represent Nakota governance and history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ansn.ca/history/makoce-land/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexis Nakota Sioux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ansn.ca/history/makoce-land/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexis Nakota Sioux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 21, 1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ansn.ca/history/makoce-land/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexis Nakota Sioux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Aranazhi (Chief Alexis) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ansn.ca/history/makoce-land/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexis Nakota Sioux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical meeting place\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Edmonton \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ansn.ca/history/makoce-land/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Alexis Nakota Sioux Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "463": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Athabasca Chipewyan First Nation identifies its people as K’ai Tailé Dené, people of the land of the willow. Its history is rooted in the Peace–Athabasca Delta, where hunting, trapping, fishing and gathering sustained families over many generations. The Nation signed Treaty 8 at Fort Chipewyan in 1899. Its own account presents that agreement within an existing cultural and territorial relationship, rather than as the beginning of the community. Dene language, inherited knowledge and responsibility for the delta remain central to the Nation’s explanation of its past and its continuing treaty relationship. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://acfn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Athabasca Chipewyan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://acfn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Athabasca Chipewyan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://acfn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Athabasca Chipewyan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Chipewyan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://acfn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Athabasca Chipewyan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People’s name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>K’ai Tailé Dené \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://acfn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Athabasca Chipewyan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "473": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Bearspaw First Nation describes a traditional territory extending from buffalo-hunting country on the Plains through the Rocky Mountain foothills and watersheds, across passes into present-day British Columbia. Chief Jacob Bearspaw, Ozija Thiba, signed Treaty 7 at Blackfoot Crossing on September 22, 1877. The Nation’s history connects it with Chiniki and Goodstoney while preserving the distinct identity of each. Stoney Nakoda language and place names carry knowledge of rivers, mountains and travel routes. The treaty and reserve lands form part of that story, within a much older Îyârhe Nakoda relationship with the mountains and Plains. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bearspawfirstnation.ca/about-us/history-of-bearspaw/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bearspaw — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 7 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bearspawfirstnation.ca/about-us/history-of-bearspaw/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bearspaw — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 22, 1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bearspawfirstnation.ca/about-us/history-of-bearspaw/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bearspaw — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Blackfoot Crossing \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bearspawfirstnation.ca/about-us/history-of-bearspaw/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bearspaw — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Jacob Bearspaw (Ozija Thiba) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bearspawfirstnation.ca/about-us/history-of-bearspaw/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bearspaw — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/chiniki/\">Chiniki\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Bearspaw and Chiniki are distinct Stoney Nakoda communities whose chiefs participated in Treaty 7. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bearspawfirstnation.ca/about-us/history-of-bearspaw/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bearspaw — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/goodstoney/\">Goodstoney\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Bearspaw’s history identifies Goodstoney as a fellow Stoney Nakoda Treaty 7 signatory. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bearspawfirstnation.ca/about-us/history-of-bearspaw/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bearspaw — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "445": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Beaver First Nation is a Dane-zaa community whose history is associated with the Fort Vermilion, Boyer River and Child Lake areas. Its community app information records Chief Ambroise Tête Noire joining Treaty 8 at Fort Vermilion on July 8, 1899. Historical administrative names included the Fort Vermilion Band of Beaver Indians and Boyer River Band before adoption of the present name in 1992. The people’s territory and land-based livelihoods extended beyond the two reserves. Dane-zaa language and Elders’ knowledge remain important records of a history older than the treaty and the band names used by officials. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverfirstnation.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverfirstnation.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 8, 1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverfirstnation.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ambroise Tête Noire \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverfirstnation.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Present name adopted\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1992 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverfirstnation.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "460": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Beaver Lake Cree Nation’s own history identifies the people as Wood Cree with longstanding connections to the Lac La Biche region. Chief Pee-Yas-See-Wah-We-Cha-Koot, also known as Pee-ay-sis, and councillor Pay-Pay-See-See-Moo signed Treaty 6 at Fort Pitt on September 9, 1876. Reserve land was not formally set apart until May 31, 1911. The Nation describes declining fish and game, epidemics and pressure to change established livelihoods during the intervening period. It also records people joining from the Lac La Biche band. These events concern treaty obligations and colonial administration within an older Cree history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverlakecreenation.ca/community/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverlakecreenation.ca/community/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 9, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverlakecreenation.ca/community/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Pitt \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverlakecreenation.ca/community/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve set apart\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>May 31, 1911 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://beaverlakecreenation.ca/community/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Beaver Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "458": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Bigstone Cree Nation joined Treaty 8 in 1899, but outstanding obligations concerning reserve land and other benefits continued for more than a century. A settlement approved in community votes in 2010 addressed treaty land entitlement and ancillary benefits. The process included members from Wabasca, Calling Lake, Chipewyan Lake, Peerless Lake and Trout Lake. One result was the separate constitution of Peerless Trout First Nation on May 19, 2010. These administrative developments did not create the communities’ Indigenous histories; they addressed aspects of an existing treaty relationship and the distinct needs of people living across the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/news/archive/2011/09/fact-sheet-settlement-agreement-bigstone-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bigstone Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/news/archive/2011/09/fact-sheet-settlement-agreement-bigstone-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bigstone Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty entry\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/news/archive/2011/09/fact-sheet-settlement-agreement-bigstone-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bigstone Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Settlement approval\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2010 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/news/archive/2011/09/fact-sheet-settlement-agreement-bigstone-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bigstone Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/peerless-trout-first-nation/\">Peerless Trout First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Peerless Trout became a separate First Nation through the Bigstone treaty land entitlement and benefits settlement process in 2010. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/news/archive/2011/09/fact-sheet-settlement-agreement-bigstone-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bigstone Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "435": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">The Blood Tribe, or Kainai, describes its relationship with the Crown through the Blackfoot Treaty of 1877, commonly called Treaty 7. Its own account explains the agreement through Innaihtsiiyissini, a Blackfoot understanding of treaty-making as a peace relationship between powerful nations. That perspective places the agreement within an existing political and cultural order, rather than treating it as the beginning of the Tribe. The Nation’s history and interpretation of treaty belong to the wider Blackfoot world while retaining a distinct Kainai identity. This account summarizes that historical relationship without turning present-day political statements into historical facts. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodtribe.org/community-notice-alberta-separatist-petition-january-30-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Blood — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 7 (Blackfoot Treaty) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodtribe.org/community-notice-alberta-separatist-petition-january-30-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Blood — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodtribe.org/community-notice-alberta-separatist-petition-january-30-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Blood — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People’s name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kainai \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodtribe.org/community-notice-alberta-separatist-petition-january-30-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Blood — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty concept\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Innaihtsiiyissini \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodtribe.org/community-notice-alberta-separatist-petition-january-30-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Blood — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "433": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Chiniki First Nation describes its people as Îyârhe Nakoda, people of the mountains, with a history of travel across the foothills and Rocky Mountain slopes into present-day Montana and British Columbia. Hunting buffalo and other game, fishing and harvesting plants were guided by land-based knowledge and ceremony. Chief John Chiniki joined Jacob Bearspaw and Jacob Goodstoney at the Treaty 7 negotiations at Blackfoot Crossing in 1877. Reserve creation later reshaped access to parts of that territory. Chiniki’s account preserves a distinct community history within the shared Stoney Nakoda world. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.chiniki.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chiniki — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 7 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.chiniki.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chiniki — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.chiniki.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chiniki — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>John Chiniki \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.chiniki.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chiniki — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Blackfoot Crossing \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.chiniki.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chiniki — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/bearspaw/\">Bearspaw\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Chiniki and Bearspaw are distinct Stoney Nakoda Nations whose chiefs signed Treaty 7 together. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.chiniki.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chiniki — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/goodstoney/\">Goodstoney\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Chiniki’s history identifies Jacob Goodstoney as another Stoney Nakoda chief at the 1877 negotiations. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.chiniki.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chiniki — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "470": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Chipewyan Prairie First Nation’s own account locates its Denésoliné history in lands radiating outward from the Christina River watershed. Hunting, gathering and other land-based activities sustained the people’s livelihood, culture and identity long before reserve administration. The reserve at Janvier is adjacent to the separate hamlet of Janvier, which also includes Métis families and other residents. These neighbouring places should not be treated as a single government or as having identical histories. The Nation describes Denésoliné as its inherited language and emphasizes continuity of land use rather than a single founding event. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://cpfn.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chipewyan Prairie First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Denésoliné \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://cpfn.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chipewyan Prairie First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Christina River watershed \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://cpfn.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chipewyan Prairie First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Administrative community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Janvier 194 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://cpfn.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chipewyan Prairie First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Denésoliné \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://cpfn.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chipewyan Prairie First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "464": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Cold Lake First Nations’ own historical account, including a narrative by John N. A. Janvier, places Denesųłiné presence far earlier than written colonial records. The Nation describes land, language, stories and teachings as inseparable, with Elders responsible for passing knowledge between generations. Its history includes the effects of imposed reserve administration and restrictions on movement, alongside the continuation of community relationships across the wider territory. The account emphasizes that land sustained the people for thousands of years. This brief preserves that perspective rather than reducing Cold Lake’s history to a treaty date or a government-created boundary. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://clfns.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cold Lake First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Denesųłiné \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://clfns.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cold Lake First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical account\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>John N. A. Janvier \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://clfns.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cold Lake First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical themes\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Land, language and Elders’ teachings \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://clfns.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cold Lake First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "448": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Dene Tha’ First Nation’s published history draws on its 1997 Traditional Land-Use and Occupancy Study. Elders’ accounts describe hunting, trapping, fishing, gathering and the rules for using land responsibly. Knowledge passed through family relationships and storytelling, rather than depending solely on written records. Around the turn of the twentieth century, trading posts and log houses contributed to more settled patterns of residence, while land-based life remained important. The study records both continuing occupation and change. Its account should therefore be read as a history told through Dene Tha’ knowledge, alongside the later administrative record. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://denetha.ca/about/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dene Tha&#x27; — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical source\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1997 Traditional Land-Use and Occupancy Study \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://denetha.ca/about/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dene Tha&#x27; — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical livelihoods\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Hunting, trapping, fishing and gathering \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://denetha.ca/about/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dene Tha&#x27; — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical change\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>More settled residence around 1900 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://denetha.ca/about/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dene Tha&#x27; — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "450": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Driftpile Cree Nation’s history is connected to Lesser Slave Lake and Chief Kinosayo’s participation in Treaty 8 in 1899. The Nation’s own timeline describes later administrative divisions of the original band and the importance of gardens, farming and household work in community life. George Okimaw is remembered for teaching agricultural practices. Women’s work included preparing hides and clothing, caring for children and managing essential household needs. These details broaden the written treaty record into a history of families and livelihoods. Uncertain stories about reserve boundaries on the source page are not repeated as established fact here. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://dcn450.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Driftpile Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://dcn450.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Driftpile Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://dcn450.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Driftpile Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kinosayo \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://dcn450.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Driftpile Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lesser Slave Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://dcn450.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Driftpile Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "451": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Duncan’s First Nation’s own history records entry into Treaty 8 on July 1, 1899. Reserve parcels were surveyed in 1905, with formal confirmations in 1907 and 1925. Most were surrendered in 1928; one parcel, 151K, was returned in 1965. These land changes are central to the community’s reserve history in the Peace River region. The account also records later regional relationships, including formation of the Western Cree Tribal Council in 1998 with Horse Lake and Sturgeon Lake. The people’s Woods Cree history extends beyond those treaty, land and organizational dates. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan&#x27;s First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan&#x27;s First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 1, 1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan&#x27;s First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1905 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan&#x27;s First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land returned\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>151K, 1965 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan&#x27;s First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/horse-lake-first-nation/\">Horse Lake First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Duncan’s and Horse Lake formed the Western Cree Tribal Council with Sturgeon Lake in 1998. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan&#x27;s First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/sturgeon-lake-cree-nation/\">Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Duncan’s history records a shared role with Sturgeon Lake in forming the Western Cree Tribal Council in 1998. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan&#x27;s First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "440": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Enoch Cree Nation’s own account records joining Treaty 6 on September 25, 1877. Its people identify with Plains Cree history and with the land west of present-day Edmonton. Stony Plain Reserve 135 was established in September 1889. The Nation describes land losses in 1902 and 1908 as forced sales and wrongful surrenders that substantially reduced its land base. Language, stories, songs, dance and ceremony preserve an identity older than those administrative events. The treaty is presented as a nation-to-nation relationship with the Crown, rather than the origin of the community. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://enochnation.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Enoch Cree Nation #440 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://enochnation.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Enoch Cree Nation #440 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 25, 1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://enochnation.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Enoch Cree Nation #440 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 1889 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://enochnation.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Enoch Cree Nation #440 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical reserve name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Stony Plain 135 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://enochnation.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Enoch Cree Nation #440 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "443": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Ermineskin Cree Nation’s own history places its people in the Bear Hills, Maskwacheesihk, for centuries before the reserve was established in 1885. It identifies Ermineskin as one of the Four Sister Nations of Maskwacis and as part of Treaty 6. The account emphasizes the continuation of Cree community identity through language, culture and the preservation of history. Agricultural land and later oil and gas resources became important in reserve-era economic life. The 1885 reserve date is an administrative milestone within that older history, not the beginning of the Nation’s connection with the Bear Hills. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ermineskin.ca/history-culture/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ermineskin Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ermineskin.ca/history-culture/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ermineskin Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1885 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ermineskin.ca/history-culture/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ermineskin Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Maskwacheesihk (Bear Hills) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ermineskin.ca/history-culture/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ermineskin Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Four Sister Nations of Maskwacis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://ermineskin.ca/history-culture/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ermineskin Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "467": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Fort McKay First Nation’s history is rooted in Cree and Dene communities along the Athabasca River and surrounding lakes. Its own account notes the Hudson’s Bay Company trading post established near the present community in 1820, a point in the written fur-trade record rather than the start of Indigenous occupation. Fort McKay is a Treaty 8 Nation. The community’s land base also includes places at Moose and Buffalo lakes, reflecting relationships beyond the main settlement. More recent industry relationships form another chapter in a history that includes land-based livelihoods and distinct Cree and Dene heritage. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortmckay.com/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McKay First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortmckay.com/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McKay First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Trading post established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1820 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortmckay.com/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McKay First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical river\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Athabasca River \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortmckay.com/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McKay First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community heritage\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cree and Dene \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortmckay.com/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McKay First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "468": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Fort McMurray #468 First Nation’s treaty-era history includes the federal creation of the Fort McMurray Cree-Chipewyan Band in 1899. The Indian Claims Commission’s inquiry explains that this grouping was administratively convenient and did not correspond to a single pre-existing community. Cree and Dene groups associated with Fort McMurray, Fort McKay and other locations retained distinct residence and land-use histories. The Treaty 8 adhesion and subsequent band administration therefore need to be distinguished from the people’s earlier identities. This account avoids treating a shared government record as proof of identical language, ancestry or governance. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/indianclaims/RC31-59-1995E.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McMurray #468 First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/indianclaims/RC31-59-1995E.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McMurray #468 First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical administrative grouping\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort McMurray Cree-Chipewyan Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/indianclaims/RC31-59-1995E.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McMurray #468 First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Grouping recorded\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/indianclaims/RC31-59-1995E.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McMurray #468 First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/fort-mckay-first-nation/\">Fort McKay First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Fort McKay and Fort McMurray were historically administered within the Fort McMurray Cree-Chipewyan Band, despite distinct community identities. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/indianclaims/RC31-59-1995E.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort McMurray #468 First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "465": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Frog Lake’s nineteenth-century history includes the severe pressures faced by Plains Cree communities after Treaty 6: disappearing bison, hunger and government withholding of promised assistance. Alberta’s historic-site account explains the killings at Frog Lake in 1885 within that wider context. It also notes that more recent research and oral histories have changed interpretations of the event. Chief Big Bear sought improved conditions and fulfilment of treaty promises. The tragedy should not be used to assign collective responsibility to today’s Frog Lake First Nation or to replace the community’s much broader history with one episode. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alberta.ca/frog-lake-historic-site\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Frog Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical treaty context\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alberta.ca/frog-lake-historic-site\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Frog Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Significant event\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Frog Lake, 1885 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alberta.ca/frog-lake-historic-site\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Frog Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical pressures\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Bison decline, hunger and withheld assistance \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.alberta.ca/frog-lake-historic-site\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Frog Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "475": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Goodstoney First Nation’s account of Goodstoney Meadows describes a gathering place used by Stoney Nakoda families since time immemorial. Families and clans returned from winter camps in spring to meet, share food and stories, and welcome warmer weather. The meadows also hosted horse racing, sports and ceremonies, including the Sundance. This seasonal gathering history connects the community with the wider Rocky Mountain landscape and with knowledge preserved through ceremony and oral tradition. It provides a different kind of historical record from treaty signatures or reserve surveys, without making those colonial documents the starting point of the people’s story. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.goodstoneynation.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Goodstoney — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Stoney Nakoda \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.goodstoneynation.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Goodstoney — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical gathering place\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Goodstoney Meadows \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.goodstoneynation.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Goodstoney — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Seasonal use\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Spring gathering after winter camps \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.goodstoneynation.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Goodstoney — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "469": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Heart Lake First Nation’s written treaty history contains conflicting administrative records. The Lac La Biche Archives’ historical study explains that federal registers variously associated Heart Lake with Treaty 6 and Treaty 8, while a recorded 1899 date was inconsistent with the actual start of Treaty 8 negotiations. A 1916 Order in Council set aside reserve land under Treaty 8 terms. The study cautions that the precise signing history is not established by those records alone. This brief preserves that uncertainty rather than assigning a confident signing date, and distinguishes administrative classification from the community’s older history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.portagecollege.ca/documents/about/college-publications/other/Lac%20La%20Biche%20Chronicles_The%20Early%20Years_2nd%20Printing%202016.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Heart Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical issue\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Conflicting treaty records \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.portagecollege.ca/documents/about/college-publications/other/Lac%20La%20Biche%20Chronicles_The%20Early%20Years_2nd%20Printing%202016.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Heart Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve Order in Council\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 20, 1916 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.portagecollege.ca/documents/about/college-publications/other/Lac%20La%20Biche%20Chronicles_The%20Early%20Years_2nd%20Printing%202016.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Heart Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lac La Biche district \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.portagecollege.ca/documents/about/college-publications/other/Lac%20La%20Biche%20Chronicles_The%20Early%20Years_2nd%20Printing%202016.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Heart Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "449": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Horse Lake First Nation’s history is connected to Dane-zaa people and a territory extending across present-day northwestern Alberta and northeastern British Columbia. The provincial consultation record identifies its predecessor as the Beaver Band at Dunvegan, which adhered to Treaty 8 on July 6, 1899. Hunting, fishing, trapping and cultural practices continued across a wider area than the reserve boundaries. The account records Dane-zaa as a traditional language. These treaty-era names describe colonial administration and should not be taken as a complete account of the community’s ancestry, oral histories or land relationships. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://projects.eao.gov.bc.ca/api/document/58923181b637cc02bea16434/fetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Horse Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://projects.eao.gov.bc.ca/api/document/58923181b637cc02bea16434/fetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Horse Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 6, 1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://projects.eao.gov.bc.ca/api/document/58923181b637cc02bea16434/fetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Horse Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Beaver Band at Dunvegan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://projects.eao.gov.bc.ca/api/document/58923181b637cc02bea16434/fetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Horse Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dane-zaa \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://projects.eao.gov.bc.ca/api/document/58923181b637cc02bea16434/fetch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Horse Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "452": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Kapawe’no First Nation’s community history is associated with Grouard in the Lesser Slave Lake region. Its own account describes a governance tradition based on hereditary leadership and remembers Frank Halcrow’s fifty years as chief, beginning in 1970 and ending with his death in January 2020. The Nation also records the development of a governance plan defining leadership responsibilities. Education includes Cree language instruction and land-based teaching, connecting institutional development with inherited knowledge. These documented leadership and educational developments form part of the Nation’s modern history; the present reserve system does not define the beginning of the people’s connection with the area. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.kapaweno.com/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kapawe&#x27;no First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Grouard \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.kapaweno.com/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kapawe&#x27;no First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Frank Halcrow \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.kapaweno.com/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kapawe&#x27;no First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Chief’s service\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1970–2020 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.kapaweno.com/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kapawe&#x27;no First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Governance tradition\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Hereditary leadership \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.kapaweno.com/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kapawe&#x27;no First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "466": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Kehewin Cree Nation’s history is recounted by former Chief Theresa Gadwa in an account preserved by the Elk Point Historical Society. She places the community’s treaty-era establishment in 1876 and describes how the reserve system changed Cree freedom of movement and political life. Her account explains the roles of the Okimaw, or chief, and councillors in dealings with Indian Affairs. It presents treaty and reserve administration as changes imposed on an existing people, rather than their origin. The perspective of a former community leader adds an important voice alongside government documents. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://elkpointhistory.ca/beginnings/regional-histories/kehewin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kehewin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://elkpointhistory.ca/beginnings/regional-histories/kehewin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kehewin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty-era record\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://elkpointhistory.ca/beginnings/regional-histories/kehewin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kehewin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical account\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Theresa Gadwa, former Chief \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://elkpointhistory.ca/beginnings/regional-histories/kehewin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kehewin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leadership term\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Okimaw \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://elkpointhistory.ca/beginnings/regional-histories/kehewin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kehewin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "447": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Little Red River Cree Nation’s treaty record includes joining Treaty 8 at the Little Red River post on July 11, 1899. The first annuity distribution there took place on the same day. North Peace Tribal Council’s account emphasizes that the Nation’s unwritten history reaches much further back and has been passed between generations through collective storytelling. Its communities include Fox Lake, John D’or Prairie and Garden River. Treaty documentation and present-day administrative arrangements therefore describe only part of a wider Cree history. Oral knowledge and the records of the different communities remain important in understanding that past. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.nptc.ca/little-red-river-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Red River Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.nptc.ca/little-red-river-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Red River Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 11, 1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.nptc.ca/little-red-river-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Red River Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Little Red River post \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.nptc.ca/little-red-river-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Red River Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical record\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Oral tradition and treaty documents \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.nptc.ca/little-red-river-cree-nation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Red River Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "476": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Loon River First Nation’s own history distinguishes the wider Treaty 8 relationship, dating to 1899, from the community’s later achievement of reserve status in 1993. Its account presents reserve recognition as a milestone in self-government and Nation-building, not the beginning of the people’s existence. Language, culture, Christian traditions and responsibility for traditional lands are identified as important parts of the community’s inherited identity. The history of formal recognition must therefore be read alongside a much older pattern of community life and land use. An exact individual adhesion date is not assigned from the general Treaty 8 date. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://loonriverfirstnation.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Loon River Cree — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://loonriverfirstnation.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Loon River Cree — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve status achieved\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1993 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://loonriverfirstnation.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Loon River Cree — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical themes\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Language, culture and traditional lands \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://loonriverfirstnation.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Loon River Cree — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "439": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Louis Bull Tribe’s history belongs to the Cree communities of Maskwacis, the Bear Hills region of central Alberta. The Tribe identifies itself as one of the Four Nations of Maskwacis. Its own public account connects the reserve system with the treaty period while emphasizing a continuing Cree community identity. More recent economic history includes the Tribe’s founding and ownership of the Bear Hills Casino and Travel Resort. That development is part of a broader effort to support employment and community services. It is a modern chapter in the Tribe’s story, rather than an explanation of the origins of its people. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://louisbulltribe.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Louis Bull Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Maskwacis (Bear Hills) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://louisbulltribe.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Louis Bull Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Four Nations of Maskwacis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://louisbulltribe.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Louis Bull Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://louisbulltribe.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Louis Bull Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Modern community enterprise\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Bear Hills Casino and Travel Resort \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://louisbulltribe.ca/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Louis Bull Tribe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "453": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Lubicon Lake Band’s modern land history includes decades of negotiation over an unresolved land claim and treaty benefits. In October 2018, the Band, Alberta and Canada announced signed agreements following a community vote supporting settlement. The agreements provided a substantial land allocation, compensation and support for community infrastructure. This settlement addressed longstanding outstanding obligations; it should not be described as the beginning of the Lubicon people or their relationship with their territory. The history also demonstrates why residence inside the geographical area of Treaty 8 does not, by itself, establish an original 1899 signing date for every community. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/crown-indigenous-relations-northern-affairs/news/2018/10/lubicon-lake-band-alberta-and-canada-celebrate-historic-land-claim-settlement.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lubicon Lake Band No. 453 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Significant agreement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Land claim and treaty benefits settlement \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/crown-indigenous-relations-northern-affairs/news/2018/10/lubicon-lake-band-alberta-and-canada-celebrate-historic-land-claim-settlement.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lubicon Lake Band No. 453 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Settlement announced\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>October 24, 2018 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/crown-indigenous-relations-northern-affairs/news/2018/10/lubicon-lake-band-alberta-and-canada-celebrate-historic-land-claim-settlement.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lubicon Lake Band No. 453 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Parties\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lubicon Lake Band, Alberta and Canada \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/crown-indigenous-relations-northern-affairs/news/2018/10/lubicon-lake-band-alberta-and-canada-celebrate-historic-land-claim-settlement.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lubicon Lake Band No. 453 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "461": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Mikisew Cree First Nation’s treaty history includes joining Treaty 8 in 1899. Its traditional territory lies largely in northeastern Alberta, where land and resource decisions have repeatedly affected the exercise of treaty rights. The Nation later challenged federal law-making that it argued could affect those rights. A Supreme Court of Canada decision in October 2018 considered the Crown’s obligations during the legislative process. That case is part of the modern history of the Nation’s defence of its treaty relationship, rather than a replacement for its older community history or an account of all its legal proceedings. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.scc-csc.ca/judgments-jugements/cb/2018/37441/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mikisew Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.scc-csc.ca/judgments-jugements/cb/2018/37441/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mikisew Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty entry\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.scc-csc.ca/judgments-jugements/cb/2018/37441/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mikisew Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Northeastern Alberta \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.scc-csc.ca/judgments-jugements/cb/2018/37441/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mikisew Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Documented court decision\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>October 11, 2018 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.scc-csc.ca/judgments-jugements/cb/2018/37441/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mikisew Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "442": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Montana First Nation’s history is rooted in Maskwacis, the Bear Hills, where Cree people gathered for social and spiritual purposes before the treaty period. The Nation’s own account explains that land initially set aside for Bobtail’s band was later granted to the Montana Band after the earlier residents dispersed. A surrender in 1909 greatly reduced that reserve. The account connects this land history with Treaty 6 and the choices made around reserve settlement. Its discussion of the surrender is a historical account; the present status of any related litigation is not inferred from an undated website statement. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://montanafirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montana — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://montanafirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montana — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Maskwacis (Bear Hills) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://montanafirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montana — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier land allocation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Bobtail Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://montanafirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montana — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Significant land surrender\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1909 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://montanafirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Montana — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "431": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">O’Chiese First Nation’s own oral-history account describes Saulteaux movement westward during the fur-trade period and families using the Baptiste River as a winter camp. Hunting and trapping supported families who also travelled south in summer. Elders’ accounts connect settlement in the area with efforts to keep children from residential schools. In 1950, a group of families joined Treaty 6 and settled on reserve land near Rocky Mountain House. The community identifies Saulteaux and Cree ancestry and Saulteaux language. These oral accounts place the twentieth-century treaty adhesion within a much older history of movement and land use. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ochiese.ca/ourcommunity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O&#x27;Chiese — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ochiese.ca/ourcommunity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O&#x27;Chiese — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1950 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ochiese.ca/ourcommunity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O&#x27;Chiese — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Saulteaux \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ochiese.ca/ourcommunity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O&#x27;Chiese — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical winter camps\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Baptiste River \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ochiese.ca/ourcommunity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O&#x27;Chiese — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "441": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Paul First Nation’s own account describes Nakoda movement over a broad territory from the Hudson Bay region toward the Rocky Mountains and south along the foothills. Connections with Cree people and participation in the fur trade formed part of that movement. One of the Stoney groups later chose land northwest of present-day Edmonton and became associated with Paul First Nation. This account emphasizes the wider landscape and relationships that preceded the reserve community. It should not be read as fixing every family’s ancestry or reducing the history of the people to a single migration, treaty or reserve date. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.paulfirstnation.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Paul — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People in the historical account\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Nakoda (Stoney) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.paulfirstnation.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Paul — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical context\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fur trade and westward movement \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.paulfirstnation.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Paul — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve-era region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Northwest of Edmonton \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.paulfirstnation.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Paul — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "478": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Peerless Trout First Nation’s community history is rooted in the lakes, muskeg and prairies around Peerless and Trout lakes. Fish and wildlife sustained families well before the modern band was constituted. The Nation was officially constituted on May 19, 2010 as part of the wider Bigstone land and treaty-benefits settlement process. Its own account explains that recognition in relation to the communities’ long struggle for land and services. The 2010 date concerns separate band administration; it does not mark the beginning of the people, their Cree identity or their relationship with the surrounding traditional territory. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ptfn.net/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peerless Trout First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Separate band constituted\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>May 19, 2010 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ptfn.net/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peerless Trout First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical communities\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Peerless Lake and Trout Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ptfn.net/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peerless Trout First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Bigstone settlement process \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ptfn.net/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peerless Trout First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/bigstone-cree-nation/\">Bigstone Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Peerless Trout’s separate band recognition arose through the Bigstone treaty land entitlement and benefits settlement. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ptfn.net/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peerless Trout First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "436": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Piikani Nation’s own account places its history within the Blackfoot Confederacy and a territory sustained by stories, ceremonies, songs and detailed land knowledge. It describes an ancestral landscape reaching north toward the North Saskatchewan River, south toward the Yellowstone country and through the Rocky Mountains. The Nation presents itself as a continuing custodian of that territory, rather than a community created by a colonial boundary. Its intergovernmental charter records that historical understanding and the responsibility to preserve knowledge across generations. This territorial account represents the Nation’s own perspective and is not presented as an exclusive modern boundary map. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://consultation.piikanination.com/charter/oki-welcome\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piikani Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Blackfoot Confederacy \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://consultation.piikanination.com/charter/oki-welcome\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piikani Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical record\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Stories, ceremonies and songs \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://consultation.piikanination.com/charter/oki-welcome\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piikani Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Territorial account\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Piikani ancestral territory \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://consultation.piikanination.com/charter/oki-welcome\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Piikani Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "462": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Saddle Lake Cree Nation’s own timeline records nineteenth-century leaders including Kehkek and Onchaminahos, or Little Hunter, and their participation in Treaty 6 in 1876. It also records the legal amalgamation of Blue Quills, Saddle Lake, Waskatenaw and Whitefish lands in 1902. The community’s later history includes a 1925 land surrender, residential schooling, restrictions on movement and the development of local institutions. The component communities’ histories should not be erased by the administrative amalgamation. Dates on the source timeline that are inconsistent with established wider chronology are not repeated here. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://slcn125.ca/departments/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saddle Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://slcn125.ca/departments/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saddle Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://slcn125.ca/departments/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saddle Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Kehkek; Onchaminahos \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://slcn125.ca/departments/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saddle Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land amalgamation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1902 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://slcn125.ca/departments/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saddle Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/whitefish-lake-first-nation-128/\">Whitefish Lake First Nation #128\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Whitefish and Saddle Lake were included in the 1902 land amalgamation recorded by Saddle Lake; their present community identities remain distinct. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://slcn125.ca/departments/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Saddle Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "444": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Samson Cree Nation’s modern history includes a sustained community investment in education. On June 23, 1980, Chief and Council established the Nipisihkopahk Iyinisiwin Trust Fund and set aside funds to support members’ educational goals. The trust’s own history explains that later leadership continued that commitment. This institutional history records a deliberate community choice to support future generations through education. It is one part of the Nation’s broader Cree history and should not be mistaken for a founding date or a complete account of its land, treaty and cultural relationships. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nitf.ca/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Samson — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical institution\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Nipisihkopahk Iyinisiwin Trust Fund \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nitf.ca/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Samson — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Established\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>June 23, 1980 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nitf.ca/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Samson — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical purpose\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Support for members’ education \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nitf.ca/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Samson — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "454": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sawridge First Nation’s own account records Chief Charles Neesotasis, also called Twin, signing Treaty 8 on the shores of Lesser Slave Lake on June 21, 1899. Its name refers to the ridged landscape of its homeland. The Nation places the treaty within a community history extending back long before the written agreement. More recent history includes economic development beginning in the early 1970s and efforts to strengthen self-determination. Neesotasis is remembered for seeking opportunities for descendants and maintaining the community’s future. The account presents treaty as an enduring relationship rather than an isolated historical transaction. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sawridgefirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sawridge First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sawridgefirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sawridge First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>June 21, 1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sawridgefirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sawridge First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lesser Slave Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sawridgefirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sawridge First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Charles Neesotasis (Twin) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sawridgefirstnation.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sawridge First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "430": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Siksika Nation’s own history describes the Siksikaitsitapi, or Blackfoot people, living across a large Plains territory since time immemorial. Its account extends that landscape from the North Saskatchewan River south toward the Yellowstone River and from the Continental Divide east into present-day Saskatchewan. Siksika is one of the distinct Nations of the Blackfoot Confederacy, alongside Kainai, Northern Piikani and Southern Piikani. Language and relationships across that territory preserve a history older than the current international boundary. This brief records that shared historical context while retaining Siksika’s separate identity and governance. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://siksikanation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Siksika Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Siksikaitsitapi (Blackfoot) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://siksikanation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Siksika Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Siksika \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://siksikanation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Siksika Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Blackfoot Confederacy \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://siksikanation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Siksika Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/blood/\">Blood\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Siksika’s own account identifies Kainai, or Blood Tribe, as a distinct member of the Blackfoot Confederacy. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://siksikanation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Siksika Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/piikani-nation/\">Piikani Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Northern Piikani is identified as another distinct Blackfoot Confederacy Nation. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://siksikanation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Siksika Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "455": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation’s treaty history includes an adhesion to Treaty 8 at Lesser Slave Lake on June 8, 1900. Commissioner James Ansdell Macrae recorded meeting Cree people from Sturgeon Lake who wished to enter the treaty. His report distinguishes this adhesion from the original treaty negotiations of 1899. These documents preserve a Crown account of the encounter and the formal agreement. They do not establish the beginning of Cree history in the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028813/1581293624572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> Later regional cooperation included the formation of the Western Cree Tribal Council with Duncan’s and Horse Lake First Nations in 1998. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan’s First Nation — regional council history\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028813/1581293624572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Adhesion date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>June 8, 1900 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028813/1581293624572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Adhesion location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lesser Slave Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028813/1581293624572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Commissioner\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>James Ansdell Macrae \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028813/1581293624572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/duncans-first-nation/\">Duncan&#x27;s First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Duncan’s history records the three Nations forming the Western Cree Tribal Council in 1998. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan’s First Nation — regional council history\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/horse-lake-first-nation/\">Horse Lake First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Horse Lake shared in forming the Western Cree Tribal Council with Sturgeon Lake and Duncan’s in 1998. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.duncansfirstnation.com/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Duncan’s First Nation — regional council history\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "456": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sucker Creek First Nation, Namipi Sipîsis, describes its Cree history in the Lesser Slave Lake region and its Treaty 8 relationship dating to 1899. Its own account emphasizes kinship, language and shared experience. The history also includes the effects of St. Bruno’s Residential School at Joussard, attended by many members before its closure, and later changes in schooling. Those experiences belong within a continuing community history rather than defining the Nation only through colonial institutions. The present reserve near Kinuso is one part of a wider Nehiyaw relationship with the land and the lake. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://scfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sucker Creek — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://scfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sucker Creek — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://scfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sucker Creek — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Namipi Sipîsis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://scfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sucker Creek — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lesser Slave Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://scfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sucker Creek — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "434": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sunchild First Nation’s own account records its Treaty 6 adhesion on May 25, 1944 under Chief Louis Sunchild. The community’s reserve, Sunchild 202, lies northwest of Rocky Mountain House and adjoins O’Chiese. The relatively recent adhesion and reserve administration should not be mistaken for the beginning of the Cree families or their relationship with the region. The source describes a continuing community with its own educational and local institutions. This brief distinguishes the specific Sunchild adhesion from the original Treaty 6 negotiations held decades earlier in 1876. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.sunchildfirstnation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sunchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 6 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.sunchildfirstnation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sunchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>May 25, 1944 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.sunchildfirstnation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sunchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Louis Sunchild \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.sunchildfirstnation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sunchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Sunchild 202 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.sunchildfirstnation.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sunchild First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "457": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Swan River First Nation’s own historical account describes Woodland Cree families around the south shore of Lesser Slave Lake. Summer lakeside settlements were connected with inland hunting and trapping camps. The people participated in the Treaty 8 process in 1899, alongside other Lesser Slave Lake communities. Fur trading, farming and changing wildlife resources shaped livelihoods during that period. The account identifies settlements at Swan River, Wahpah and Assineau River, placing the reserve community within a wider seasonal landscape. Treaty and reserve boundaries therefore record only part of the history of movement and land use. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://srfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://srfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://srfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Woodland Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://srfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>South shore of Lesser Slave Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://srfn.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "446": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Tallcree Tribal Government’s own account places Woodland Cree families along the Peace River and in the northern boreal forest over many generations. Seasonal movement, hunting, fishing and trapping sustained community life and Cree teachings. Tallcree joined Treaty 8 in 1899. The account describes subsequent disruption from settlement, government restrictions and residential schools, alongside the persistence of identity. South Tallcree near Fort Vermilion became the first recognized reserve community; families later moved north closer to traplines, forming North Tallcree. These administrative and settlement changes sit within an older history of land use. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.tallcree.ca/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tallcree Tribal Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.tallcree.ca/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tallcree Tribal Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1899 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.tallcree.ca/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tallcree Tribal Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Woodland Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.tallcree.ca/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tallcree Tribal Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical communities\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>South Tallcree; North Tallcree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.tallcree.ca/our-story/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tallcree Tribal Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "432": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Tsuut’ina Nation’s own account emphasizes a distinct Dene-speaking identity connected with a wider history of language, culture, customs and territory. The people were formerly called Sarcee or Sarsi in colonial records. Head Chief Chiila, or Bullhead, entered Treaty 7 with the Crown in 1877. The Nation interprets that relationship through its own laws and teachings while maintaining the autonomy of its identity and governance. Oral histories explain the people’s earlier movement and relationships. The treaty date therefore belongs within a continuing Tsuut’ina history rather than marking the origin of the Nation. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://tsuutina.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tsuut&#x27;ina Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 7 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://tsuutina.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tsuut&#x27;ina Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty year\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://tsuutina.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tsuut&#x27;ina Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Chiila (Bullhead) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://tsuutina.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tsuut&#x27;ina Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical outside names\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Sarcee; Sarsi \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://tsuutina.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tsuut&#x27;ina Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "477": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Tthebatthie Denesųłiné Nation, historically recorded as Smith’s Landing First Nation, is connected with the Thebathi Dénésųłiné of the South Slave region. The Fort Smith community profile identifies the people as Treaty 8 signatories with longstanding use of lands and waters across the northern region. Its account describes Dene Ch’anié, the path we walk, as a guide to conduct and responsibility toward the land. Reserve lands lie in Alberta and Wood Buffalo National Park, while community relationships also extend into Fort Smith. Modern provincial and territorial boundaries do not contain the whole of this history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortsmith.ca/media/1696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tthebatthie Denesųłiné Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortsmith.ca/media/1696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tthebatthie Denesųłiné Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Smith’s Landing First Nation \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortsmith.ca/media/1696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tthebatthie Denesųłiné Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>South Slave \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortsmith.ca/media/1696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tthebatthie Denesųłiné Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Cultural teaching\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dene Ch’anié \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.fortsmith.ca/media/1696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tthebatthie Denesųłiné Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "459": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Whitefish Lake First Nation at Atikameg has a treaty history that requires care with the distinction between signing and administration. A historical study of communities left out of the original Treaty 8 process records Whitefish Lake people appearing on the 1901 annuity paylist and receiving arrears for earlier years. The study notes uncertainty about representation at the original negotiations and the absence of a designated chief or headman in those records. This account therefore does not assign a confident 1899 signing date. Whitefish Lake 459 is distinct from Whitefish Lake 128 at Goodfish Lake. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.collectionscanada.gc.ca/obj/s4/f2/dsk3/SSU/TC-SSU-07102007091310.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty context\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.collectionscanada.gc.ca/obj/s4/f2/dsk3/SSU/TC-SSU-07102007091310.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Documented paylist\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1901 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.collectionscanada.gc.ca/obj/s4/f2/dsk3/SSU/TC-SSU-07102007091310.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Atikameg \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.collectionscanada.gc.ca/obj/s4/f2/dsk3/SSU/TC-SSU-07102007091310.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical issue\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Uncertain original treaty representation \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.collectionscanada.gc.ca/obj/s4/f2/dsk3/SSU/TC-SSU-07102007091310.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "ab-whitefish-lake-128": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Whitefish Lake First Nation #128’s own historical submission describes ancestors occupying the North Saskatchewan River watershed before European arrival. Families hunted, trapped and fished under their own laws and customs. During the fur trade, they interacted with traders at places including Fort Edmonton and Fort Augustus near the Sturgeon River’s mouth. The account connects the present Goodfish Lake community with that wider land-use history, rather than restricting its past to the reserve boundary. Whitefish Lake 128 should be distinguished from Whitefish Lake 459 at Atikameg, despite their similar English names. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://registrydocumentsprd.blob.core.windows.net/commentsblob/project-90123/comment-64342/Whitefish%20Lake%20%23128%20Comments%20on%20the%20IPD%20for%20Beacon%20AI%20Centers%20Heartland%20Project%20%282026-01-26%29.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake First Nation #128 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical people\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Plains Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://registrydocumentsprd.blob.core.windows.net/commentsblob/project-90123/comment-64342/Whitefish%20Lake%20%23128%20Comments%20on%20the%20IPD%20for%20Beacon%20AI%20Centers%20Heartland%20Project%20%282026-01-26%29.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake First Nation #128 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>North Saskatchewan River watershed \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://registrydocumentsprd.blob.core.windows.net/commentsblob/project-90123/comment-64342/Whitefish%20Lake%20%23128%20Comments%20on%20the%20IPD%20for%20Beacon%20AI%20Centers%20Heartland%20Project%20%282026-01-26%29.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake First Nation #128 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Fur-trade places\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Edmonton; Fort Augustus \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://registrydocumentsprd.blob.core.windows.net/commentsblob/project-90123/comment-64342/Whitefish%20Lake%20%23128%20Comments%20on%20the%20IPD%20for%20Beacon%20AI%20Centers%20Heartland%20Project%20%282026-01-26%29.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake First Nation #128 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Goodfish Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://registrydocumentsprd.blob.core.windows.net/commentsblob/project-90123/comment-64342/Whitefish%20Lake%20%23128%20Comments%20on%20the%20IPD%20for%20Beacon%20AI%20Centers%20Heartland%20Project%20%282026-01-26%29.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Whitefish Lake First Nation #128 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "474": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Woodland Cree First Nation’s own account records signing a Treaty 8 adhesion on September 24, 1991. Its communities and reserve lands are associated with Cadotte, Simon, Golden and Marten lakes northeast of Peace River. The comparatively recent treaty adhesion belongs to the history of formal recognition and community administration, rather than the beginning of the people’s Cree identity or relationship with the region. The Nation describes a commitment to a self-supporting community that respects people, the environment and neighbouring communities. Its several settlements should not be reduced to a single village or treated as newly created by the adhesion. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.woodlandcree.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Woodland Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 8 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.woodlandcree.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Woodland Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 24, 1991 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.woodlandcree.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Woodland Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical communities\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cadotte, Simon, Golden and Marten lakes \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.woodlandcree.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Woodland Cree First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "308": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Barren Lands First Nation is associated with Brochet and Reserve 197 on the northern shore of Reindeer Lake. Keewatin Tribal Council identifies Cree and Dene as the community’s Indigenous languages and records its Treaty 10 relationship. Part of the earlier Barren Lands band later separated to form Northlands First Nation and moved to Lac Brochet, farther north. This shared administrative history helps explain the relationship between the two present-day Nations. The division of a band and the establishment of reserves are milestones in government records, rather than the beginning of the peoples’ relationship with the Reindeer Lake region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/barren-lands-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Barren Lands — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 10 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/barren-lands-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Barren Lands — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Brochet \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/barren-lands-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Barren Lands — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Indigenous languages\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cree and Dene \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/barren-lands-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Barren Lands — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/northlands-denesuline-first-nation/\">Northlands Denesuline First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Northlands separated from the earlier Barren Lands band and relocated to Lac Brochet. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/barren-lands-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Barren Lands — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "266": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Berens River’s recent history includes a sustained effort to bring education services under community direction. Its Student Services account traces the initiative to 2003, when Chief Alfred Everett began proceedings to transfer services from Southeast Tribal Council. Chief Nancy Everett subsequently negotiated and signed the transfer agreements. The Nation describes this as a step toward self-governance. Its account also recalls the sacrifices of families whose children had to leave home to continue secondary school. This history of education and community responsibility forms one part of Berens River’s broader story, preserved through the Mino-Kii-Kii-mah ghe-win documentary. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.berensriver.ca/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Berens River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community initiative\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Student Services, 2003 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.berensriver.ca/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Berens River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leaders\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Alfred Everett and Nancy Everett \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.berensriver.ca/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Berens River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>History project\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Mino-Kii-Kii-mah ghe-win documentary \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.berensriver.ca/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Berens River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "284": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Birdtail Sioux identifies itself as Chan Kagha Otina Dakhóta Oyáte, a Dakota Nation with an ancestral connection to its territory. Its own historical timeline distinguishes that relationship from the later Canadian administration of reserve lands. On January 4, 1873, an Order in Council authorized lands to be set aside for Dakota communities, including Birdtail Sioux. The Nation’s account emphasizes that this did not constitute a treaty with Canada. Its history therefore should not be presented as a numbered-treaty signing story. The timeline also records later community development, including the opening of a health centre in 2009. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://birdtailsioux.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birdtail Sioux — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dakota \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://birdtailsioux.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birdtail Sioux — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve-land authorization\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>January 4, 1873 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://birdtailsioux.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birdtail Sioux — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty status\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Not a numbered-treaty signatory \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://birdtailsioux.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birdtail Sioux — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Health centre milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2009 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://birdtailsioux.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birdtail Sioux — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "260": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Black River First Nation appears in earlier federal records as the Little Black River Band. A Canada Gazette order records that its council had been placed under Indian Act election provisions in March 1952. A band council resolution dated September 7, 2005 changed the name to Black River First Nation. In March 2017 the council requested a move to the First Nations Elections Act, followed by the federal order registered that May. These documented changes describe the history of the Nation’s official name and election framework; they do not mark the origin of the community or its Indigenous identity. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://gazette.gc.ca/rp-pr/p2/2017/2017-06-14/html/sor-dors98-eng.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier recorded name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Little Black River Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://gazette.gc.ca/rp-pr/p2/2017/2017-06-14/html/sor-dors98-eng.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Name-change resolution\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 7, 2005 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://gazette.gc.ca/rp-pr/p2/2017/2017-06-14/html/sor-dors98-eng.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Election-framework change\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2017 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://gazette.gc.ca/rp-pr/p2/2017/2017-06-14/html/sor-dors98-eng.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Black River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "267": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Bloodvein River First Nation describes generations of Anishinaabe life along the river, where fishing, hunting, gathering and travel sustained families. The Nation records that Sakachewayas signed Treaty 5 as Chief of Bloodvein on July 26, 1876. Its history names the Stomach (Green), Skye (Young), Benson, Turtle and Swift families among those recorded in the late nineteenth century. Elders’ teachings and seasonal harvesting continue to connect members with this history. Through its Miskwewesibi Roots Program, the Nation gathers photographs, family histories and community stories so that this knowledge can be carried forward to younger generations. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodveinriver.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bloodvein — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodveinriver.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bloodvein — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 26, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodveinriver.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bloodvein — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Sakachewayas \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodveinriver.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bloodvein — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Anishinaabemowin name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Miskwewesibi \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://bloodveinriver.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bloodvein — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "261": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Brokenhead Ojibway Nation identifies itself as an Ojibway community and a Treaty 1 Nation. Its reserve extends toward Lake Winnipeg and includes part of the Netley Creek marsh area, with the Brokenhead River running through the community. The Nation’s published vision places Ojibway identity alongside community independence and development. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://brokenheadojibwaynation.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Brokenhead Ojibway Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> Its treaty relationship belongs to the history of Treaty 1, originally signed at Lower Fort Garry on August 3, 1871. That agreement is a historical milestone within a much longer Indigenous history. The river, lake and marsh remain important geographical context for understanding the community’s place in southeastern Manitoba. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/peguis-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty One — Treaty 1 context\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 1 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://brokenheadojibwaynation.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Brokenhead Ojibway Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty’s original signing\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 3, 1871 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/peguis-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty One — Treaty 1 context\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical treaty location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Lower Fort Garry \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/peguis-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty One — Treaty 1 context\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "265": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Buffalo Point’s own history describes the Point and Reed River as gathering places and travel routes used by Anishinaabe people. Chief Ayashwash signed Treaty 3 for Buffalo Point at the Northwest Angle in 1873. Flooding in 1890 affected the reserve; a later exchange for lands at Reed River was completed in 1930. The Nation’s account follows successive generations of Thunder family leadership and the development of tourism during the 1970s. These events connect the treaty period, changes to the land base and later economic development. They form a selected account of a community whose relationship with the area predates the treaty. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.buffalopoint-firstnation.ca/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo Point First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 3 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.buffalopoint-firstnation.ca/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo Point First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ayashwash \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.buffalopoint-firstnation.ca/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo Point First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty signing\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Northwest Angle, 1873 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.buffalopoint-firstnation.ca/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo Point First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reed River land exchange\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Completed in 1930 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.buffalopoint-firstnation.ca/history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo Point First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "301": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Bunibonibee Cree Nation’s published community history includes Oxford House’s role in the fur trade. Manitoba Keewatinowi Okimakanak describes the establishment of a Hudson’s Bay Company post there in 1798 and the later influence of the United Church. These are milestones in the written history of the settlement, rather than the beginning of Cree life in the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://mkonation.com/bunibonibee-cree-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bunibonibee Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> Keewatin Tribal Council records Bunibonibee’s adhesion to Treaty 5 in 1909. The community is associated with Oxford Lake and the Hayes River, waterways that help explain its position within the northern trading and transportation network. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/bunibonibee-cree-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keewatin Tribal Council\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Oxford House \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://mkonation.com/bunibonibee-cree-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bunibonibee Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Trading-post milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1798 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://mkonation.com/bunibonibee-cree-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Bunibonibee Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 adhesion, 1909 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/bunibonibee-cree-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keewatin Tribal Council\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "289": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Canupawakpa’s history is part of the wider story of Dakota communities in the northern Plains. A historical account published by Prairie History follows Dakota movement into what is now southwestern Manitoba and the establishment of the community also recorded as Oak Lake Sioux. The Nation’s Dakota identity and relationship with its lands should be distinguished from the histories of neighbouring numbered-treaty bands. The older name is useful when reading archival records, but does not describe a separate present-day Nation. Published accounts form only one part of this history, alongside the family knowledge and oral traditions held by the community. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://prairiehistory.ca/2023/01/18/the-history-of-the-canadian-sioux-the-canupawakpa-dakota/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canupawakpa Dakota First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dakota \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://prairiehistory.ca/2023/01/18/the-history-of-the-canadian-sioux-the-canupawakpa-dakota/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canupawakpa Dakota First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier recorded name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Oak Lake Sioux \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://prairiehistory.ca/2023/01/18/the-history-of-the-canadian-sioux-the-canupawakpa-dakota/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canupawakpa Dakota First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "309": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Chemawawin Cree Nation describes a history deeply changed by the Grand Rapids hydroelectric project. Families had lived near the meeting of the Saskatchewan River and Cedar Lake, supported by the territory’s resources. Planning for the dam led to negotiations recorded in the 1962 Letter of Intent and the relocation of the community to another site on Cedar Lake. The Nation’s account describes major environmental, economic and social losses following that move. A November 9, 1990 settlement with Manitoba Hydro and later arrangements in 2004 and 2011 addressed aspects of the impacts. The Nation presents restoration as a continuing effort. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://chemawawin.com/history-of-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chemawawin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical agreement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1962 Letter of Intent \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://chemawawin.com/history-of-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chemawawin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Settlement agreement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>November 9, 1990 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://chemawawin.com/history-of-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chemawawin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Saskatchewan River and Cedar Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://chemawawin.com/history-of-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Chemawawin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "276": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Cross Lake’s modern history includes the struggle over the effects of hydroelectric development on Pimicikamak lands and waters. In evidence to a parliamentary committee, community representatives described the Northern Flood Agreement and argued that its promises had not been fulfilled. Their testimony connected environmental changes with damage to livelihoods and community well-being. This record preserves the community’s position within a wider debate about hydro development and government responsibility. It is a selected account of a modern historical issue, rather than a complete history of Pimicikamak or a substitute for the knowledge held by its people. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ourcommons.ca/Content/Committee/361/AAND/Evidence/EV1039385/aandev57-e.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cross Lake Band of Indians — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical issue\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Hydroelectric development \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ourcommons.ca/Content/Committee/361/AAND/Evidence/EV1039385/aandev57-e.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cross Lake Band of Indians — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Documented agreement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Northern Flood Agreement \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ourcommons.ca/Content/Committee/361/AAND/Evidence/EV1039385/aandev57-e.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cross Lake Band of Indians — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Pimicikamak \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ourcommons.ca/Content/Committee/361/AAND/Evidence/EV1039385/aandev57-e.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Cross Lake Band of Indians — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "288": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Dakota Plains and Dakota Tipi share an administrative history connected with the Sioux Village settlement near Portage la Prairie. Dakota Tipi’s published account records the division of that settlement in 1972 into the two present-day First Nations. Dakota Plains is associated with lands adjoining Long Plain First Nation. This band separation was an administrative milestone within a longer Dakota history. The Sioux Village name connects older records with both communities. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Plains — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dakota \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Plains — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Shared earlier settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Sioux Village \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Plains — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band division\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1972 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Plains — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/dakota-tipi/\">Dakota Tipi\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Dakota Plains and Dakota Tipi emerged as separate First Nations following the 1972 division of Sioux Village. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Plains — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "295": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Dakota Tipi First Nation’s own account records its creation as a separate First Nation in 1972, when the Sioux Village settlement near Portage la Prairie divided into Dakota Tipi and Dakota Plains. Dakota Tipi received reserve status that year on an initial land base of approximately 25 acres. The account describes later additions to those lands. These changes concern reserve administration and land holdings, rather than the beginning of the people’s Dakota history. The earlier Sioux Village name and the relationship with Dakota Plains are useful context when following the community through older government and settlement records. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Tipi — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dakota \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Tipi — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve-status milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1972 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Tipi — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Sioux Village \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.dakotatipi.ca/history.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dakota Tipi — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "316": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Dauphin River First Nation experienced a major disruption when flooding in 2011 forced residents from their homes. Manitoba’s account of the community’s rebuilt school places it within Operation Return Home, the effort to restore communities affected by the floods. The school opened in 2018, providing a local place for students after years of displacement and recovery. This milestone illustrates the importance of rebuilding community institutions as well as houses and infrastructure. The flood and return-home process are part of Dauphin River’s recent history, rather than a full account of the Nation’s older relationship with its territory. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/?item=44798\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dauphin River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Flood evacuation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2011 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/?item=44798\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dauphin River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>School reopening\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2018 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/?item=44798\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dauphin River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Recovery initiative\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Operation Return Home \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/?item=44798\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Dauphin River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "280": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Ebb and Flow First Nation’s historical lands include a medicine wheel near Alonsa. A University of Winnipeg account describes how this place inspired artwork at the university, connecting the design with Indigenous history and knowledge. The installation carries elements of the medicine wheel’s pattern into a contemporary public setting. This is one documented example of the community’s historical relationship with places beyond the boundaries of its present settlement. The account provides a cultural and territorial milestone without treating the university artwork as the origin or full expression of the teachings connected with the original site. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://news.uwinnipeg.ca/new-artwork-on-campus-inspired-by-ancient-indigenous-medicine-wheel/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ebb and Flow — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical place\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Medicine wheel near Alonsa \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://news.uwinnipeg.ca/new-artwork-on-campus-inspired-by-ancient-indigenous-medicine-wheel/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ebb and Flow — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Published account\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>University of Winnipeg, 2017 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://news.uwinnipeg.ca/new-artwork-on-campus-inspired-by-ancient-indigenous-medicine-wheel/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Ebb and Flow — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "264": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Fisher River Cree Nation traces its community history to Cree families at Norway House whose livelihoods were affected by changes in the fur trade and transportation. Chief David Rundle and other representatives sought support for a move south in 1874. The Nation’s ancestors participated in Treaty 5 in 1875, and 43 families travelled to Fisher River in 1877 after a smallpox quarantine delayed the move. Families built homes, farmed and continued fishing, trapping and seasonal work. Additional families joined through the 1908 treaty adhesion. The Nation’s account emphasizes the continuing importance of language, traditions and fishing across generations. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fisherriver.ca/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fisher River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5, 1875 and 1908 adhesion \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fisherriver.ca/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fisher River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>David Rundle \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fisherriver.ca/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fisher River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Move to Fisher River\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1877 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fisherriver.ca/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fisher River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/norway-house-cree-nation/\">Norway House Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Fisher River’s history records the 1877 move of families from Norway House. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fisherriver.ca/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fisher River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "262": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sagkeeng’s Anicinabe history is rooted in the Winnipeg River and Traverse Bay area. Its own account records ancestors’ participation in Treaty 1 in 1871 and the survey of Fort Alexander Indian Reserve in 1874. The older Fort Alexander name appears in many government and school records. The Nation’s timeline also documents the Fort Alexander residential school and the later development of community schools, including the Anicinabe Community School in 1975 and a new school in 2019. These events show changes in colonial administration and community institutions within a much longer history of Anicinabe life in the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sagkeeng.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 1, 1871 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sagkeeng.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sagkeeng.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical reserve name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fort Alexander \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sagkeeng.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community school\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1975 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sagkeeng.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fort Alexander — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "305": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Fox Lake Cree Nation’s history includes settlement around Fox Lake, Bird and Gillam along the Hudson Bay railway. Keewatin Tribal Council records federal recognition as a separate band in 1947, following separation from the York Factory First Nation. Its Treaty 5 relationship comes through the parent band’s 1910 adhesion. Bird became a reserve in 1985 and a home community was established there, while members also continued living in Gillam. These milestones explain the Nation’s connections to several communities and to York Factory. Band recognition and reserve creation describe administrative changes within a longer Cree history in northern Manitoba. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/fox-lake-cree-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fox Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Separate-band recognition\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1947 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/fox-lake-cree-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fox Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5, through the 1910 adhesion \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/fox-lake-cree-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fox Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Bird reserve milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1985 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/fox-lake-cree-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fox Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/york-factory-first-nation/\">York Factory First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Fox Lake became a separate band from York Factory in 1947. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/fox-lake-cree-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Fox Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "294": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Gambler First Nation’s own history page connects the Nation’s name with Atakawinin Tanner, known as The Gambler. It identifies him as the grandson of John “Falcon” Tanner and the son of Chief Picheeto, or Edward Tanner. These family relationships provide a starting point for reading the community’s written history. The Nation also makes available The Silver Creek, a historical report that places the band within the treaty-era history of the region. These published materials preserve selected family and archival accounts; they do not replace the wider community knowledge carried by Gambler members. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.gamblerfirstnation.ca/about-gfn/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Gambler First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Namesake\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Atakawinin Tanner, The Gambler \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.gamblerfirstnation.ca/about-gfn/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Gambler First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical family\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Picheeto (Edward) Tanner and John “Falcon” Tanner \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.gamblerfirstnation.ca/about-gfn/our-history\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Gambler First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "297": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Garden Hill’s history is linked with the earlier Island Lake band and its 1909 adhesion to Treaty 5. Four Arrows Regional Health Authority records that Garden Hill, Wasagamack, St. Theresa Point and Red Sucker Lake remained within a single band until 1969. The later division established separate First Nation administrations without erasing their shared regional history. Garden Hill is associated with the northern shore of Island Lake, and the source identifies the local language as the Island Lake Oji-Cree dialect. The treaty and band-division dates are selected administrative milestones within the much longer history of the Island Lake people. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/garden-hill-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Garden Hill First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 adhesion, 1909 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/garden-hill-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Garden Hill First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band division\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1969 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/garden-hill-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Garden Hill First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Island Lake Oji-Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/garden-hill-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Garden Hill First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/wasagamack-first-nation/\">Wasagamack First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Garden Hill and Wasagamack belonged to the earlier Island Lake band until 1969. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/garden-hill-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Garden Hill First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/st-theresa-point/\">St. Theresa Point\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Garden Hill and St. Theresa Point share the earlier Island Lake band history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/garden-hill-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Garden Hill First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/red-sucker-lake/\">Red Sucker Lake\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Garden Hill and Red Sucker Lake share the earlier Island Lake band history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/garden-hill-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Garden Hill First Nations — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "296": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">God’s Lake First Nation’s treaty history includes the 1909 adhesion to Treaty 5, as recorded by Keewatin Tribal Council. God’s Lake Narrows is part of a wider northern Cree history associated with the lake and its waterways. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/gods-lake-narrows-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: God&#x27;s Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> A separate council account describes people moving from God’s Lake Narrows to God’s River in the mid-1940s; that community later became Manto Sipi Cree Nation. The source reports differing explanations for the move, so no single cause is assumed here. These treaty and community-formation milestones help distinguish the related Nations while recognizing their shared earlier administrative history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/manto-sipi-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keewatin Tribal Council — Manto Sipi\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 adhesion, 1909 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/gods-lake-narrows-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: God&#x27;s Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>God’s Lake Narrows \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/gods-lake-narrows-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: God&#x27;s Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/manto-sipi-cree-nation/\">Manto Sipi Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>God’s River, now Manto Sipi, developed from the earlier God’s Lake community. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/manto-sipi-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keewatin Tribal Council — Manto Sipi\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "263": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Hollow Water First Nation’s published community account identifies Anishinabe as its Indigenous language and records a land-based economy that includes fishing, hunting, trapping and wild rice harvesting. These activities provide context for understanding the community’s relationship with its territory. The Nation also lists community institutions, ceremony grounds and the Wanipigow Producer Co-op. Its account emphasizes the preservation of cultural heritage alongside community well-being. This brief draws on that published description of cultural and economic continuity; it does not assign an unverified founding date, treaty-signing leader or origin story to the Nation. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://hollowwater.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hollow Water — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Indigenous language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Anishinabe \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://hollowwater.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hollow Water — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land-based activities\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Fishing, hunting, trapping and wild rice harvesting \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://hollowwater.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hollow Water — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Recorded reserve\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>I.R. 10 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://hollowwater.ca/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Hollow Water — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "286": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Keeseekoowenin Ojibway First Nation’s history is connected with Riding Mountain and the earlier Okanese band. Its account records Mekis succeeding Okanese and signing Treaty 2 in 1871. After Mekis died in 1874, his younger half-brother Keeseekoowenin, also known as Moses Burns, became Chief and served until 1906. The history describes community life through hunting, livestock, agriculture and travel routes in the Riding Mountain region. It also recognizes displacement associated with the creation of parks. These leadership and treaty milestones are part of a much longer Anishinabe relationship with the land, lakes and rivers. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://keeseekoowenin.wordpress.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoowenin — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 2, 1871 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://keeseekoowenin.wordpress.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoowenin — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty-era Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Mekis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://keeseekoowenin.wordpress.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoowenin — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Later Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Keeseekoowenin (Moses Burns), 1874–1906 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://keeseekoowenin.wordpress.com/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keeseekoowenin — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "268": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Kinonjeoshtegon First Nation is a Chippewa community in Manitoba’s Interlake region, associated with the Jackhead reserves. The older Jackhead name appears in reserve records and remains useful when tracing the community through historical documents. Interlake Reserves Tribal Council identifies Jackhead 43 and 43A as the Nation’s reserve lands. Its published profile supplies community and institutional context, rather than a detailed chronology of treaty-era leaders or migration. This brief therefore preserves the documented identity and place names without assigning an unverified founding date. The Nation’s history extends beyond these reserve designations and administrative records. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://irtc.ca/kinonjeoshtegon-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinonjeoshtegon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Chippewa \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://irtc.ca/kinonjeoshtegon-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinonjeoshtegon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical place name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Jackhead \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://irtc.ca/kinonjeoshtegon-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinonjeoshtegon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Recorded reserves\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Jackhead 43 and 43A \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://irtc.ca/kinonjeoshtegon-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Kinonjeoshtegon First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "271": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Lake Manitoba First Nation is associated with Dog Creek Reserve 46 on the northeastern shore of the lake’s southern basin. A federal community profile records its participation in Treaty 2, signed in 1871, and identifies Ojibway as the community’s Indigenous language. The Dog Creek name is an important link between older reserve records and the present-day Nation. These records describe a treaty relationship and reserve geography rather than the beginning of the people’s history. They provide a starting point for understanding Lake Manitoba’s place within the Interlake region while leaving unverified leaders, migration dates and origin stories unspecified. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1997-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lake Manitoba — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 2, 1871 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1997-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lake Manitoba — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical reserve\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dog Creek 46 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1997-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lake Manitoba — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ojibway \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1997-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lake Manitoba — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "275": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Lake St. Martin First Nation experienced prolonged displacement following the 2011 Interlake flood. Research with Elders records the effects of evacuation on health, community life and the ability to practise land-based ways of living. The study places Elders’ understanding of minoaywin, or well-being, at the centre of recovery and healing. It shows that displacement concerns relationships with people and territory as well as the loss of houses. This account documents one major event in the Nation’s recent history and the knowledge shared by participants; it is not a complete account of Lake St. Martin’s older Indigenous history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/canadian-journal-on-aging-la-revue-canadienne-du-vieillissement/article/reconciling-with-minoaywin-first-nations-elders-advice-to-promote-healing-from-forced-displacement/0B8BCF0FB395793192B57C7E9892FAD0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lake St. Martin — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical event\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2011 Interlake flood \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/canadian-journal-on-aging-la-revue-canadienne-du-vieillissement/article/reconciling-with-minoaywin-first-nations-elders-advice-to-promote-healing-from-forced-displacement/0B8BCF0FB395793192B57C7E9892FAD0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lake St. Martin — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Documented experience\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Evacuation and prolonged displacement \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/canadian-journal-on-aging-la-revue-canadienne-du-vieillissement/article/reconciling-with-minoaywin-first-nations-elders-advice-to-promote-healing-from-forced-displacement/0B8BCF0FB395793192B57C7E9892FAD0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Lake St. Martin — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "270": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Little Grand Rapids is an Anishinaabe community with ancestral lands on both sides of the Manitoba–Ontario boundary. Pimachiowin Aki’s community account describes longstanding canoe routes, family harvesting areas and the preservation of named places through Elders’ recorded knowledge. Community members are documenting Anishinaabemowin place names and their meanings so that this history remains available to future generations. The account also emphasizes that cultural places carry responsibilities and that some sacred areas should not be photographed. This history centres on living relationships with the land, language and waterways, rather than treating a reserve designation as the beginning of the community. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/little-grand-rapids-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Grand Rapids — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Berens River and Family Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/little-grand-rapids-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Grand Rapids — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language heritage\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Anishinaabemowin \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/little-grand-rapids-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Grand Rapids — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>History initiative\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Traditional named-places mapping \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/little-grand-rapids-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Grand Rapids — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "274": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Little Saskatchewan First Nation was among the Interlake communities displaced by the 2011 flood. Research with Elders examines how forced displacement disrupted everyday relationships, cultural practices and well-being. Participants’ accounts stress the importance of community, land and traditional life in healing after evacuation. Their testimony helps document the human effects of flooding beyond damage to buildings and infrastructure. This is one part of Little Saskatchewan’s recent history, presented through the study’s community-based perspective. It does not establish a founding date or substitute for the Nation’s broader oral histories. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/canadian-journal-on-aging-la-revue-canadienne-du-vieillissement/article/reconciling-with-minoaywin-first-nations-elders-advice-to-promote-healing-from-forced-displacement/0B8BCF0FB395793192B57C7E9892FAD0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Saskatchewan — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical event\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2011 Interlake flood \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/canadian-journal-on-aging-la-revue-canadienne-du-vieillissement/article/reconciling-with-minoaywin-first-nations-elders-advice-to-promote-healing-from-forced-displacement/0B8BCF0FB395793192B57C7E9892FAD0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Saskatchewan — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Documented experience\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Displacement and recovery \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/canadian-journal-on-aging-la-revue-canadienne-du-vieillissement/article/reconciling-with-minoaywin-first-nations-elders-advice-to-promote-healing-from-forced-displacement/0B8BCF0FB395793192B57C7E9892FAD0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Little Saskatchewan — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "287": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Long Plain belongs to the Treaty 1 Nations, whose treaty relationship dates to 1871. A federal profile records the settlement of its outstanding treaty land entitlement on August 4, 1994. The Nation’s reserve lands are associated with the Assiniboine River near Portage la Prairie. These milestones connect treaty obligations with later efforts to secure land and support community development. They describe selected changes in administration and land holdings within a much longer Indigenous history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2004-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Long Plain — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 1, 1871 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2004-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Long Plain — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land-entitlement settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 4, 1994 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2004-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Long Plain — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Assiniboine River \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2004-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Long Plain — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "302": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Manto Sipi Cree Nation’s history is connected with the earlier God’s Lake First Nation. Keewatin Tribal Council records that people moved from God’s Lake Narrows to establish the God’s River community in the mid-1940s. Its account notes different explanations for the separation, so the move is not assigned a single cause here. A ministerial order established God’s River First Nation on May 7, 1976. The Nation’s Treaty 5 relationship comes through the 1909 adhesion. These milestones distinguish the community’s formation and later band recognition from the longer Cree history of the lake and river region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/manto-sipi-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Manto Sipi Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 adhesion, 1909 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/manto-sipi-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Manto Sipi Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Community move\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Mid-1940s \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/manto-sipi-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Manto Sipi Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Separate-band order\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>May 7, 1976 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/manto-sipi-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Manto Sipi Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "328": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Marcel Colomb First Nation shares an earlier band history with Mathias Colomb. The Treaty Relations Commission of Manitoba records that a group of Mathias Colomb members voted in 1972 to establish a separate band and council. Official recognition of Marcel Colomb followed in 1995. This history links the Nation with Treaty 6 through its earlier community relationship. The years of the vote and recognition describe a process of administrative separation; they do not mark the beginning of Cree identity or of families’ relationships with northern Manitoba. The two present-day Nations each maintain their own community and governance. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://trcm.ca/lets-talk-treaties/treaties-with-the-crown/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Marcel Colomb First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Separation vote\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1972 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://trcm.ca/lets-talk-treaties/treaties-with-the-crown/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Marcel Colomb First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Official recognition\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1995 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://trcm.ca/lets-talk-treaties/treaties-with-the-crown/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Marcel Colomb First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier band relationship\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Mathias Colomb \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://trcm.ca/lets-talk-treaties/treaties-with-the-crown/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Marcel Colomb First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/mathias-colomb/\">Mathias Colomb\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Marcel Colomb’s separate-band process began with a 1972 vote by members of Mathias Colomb. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://trcm.ca/lets-talk-treaties/treaties-with-the-crown/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Marcel Colomb First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "311": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Mathias Colomb Cree Nation’s own timeline describes a history rooted in the Churchill River region. In 1910, following separation from the Peter Ballantyne band at Pelican Narrows, the community settled along the river at Highrock Lake. The account identifies Mathias Colomb as the first Chief, serving until his death in 1932. Prayer River was surveyed as a reserve under the band on August 29, 1926. A later fire destroyed that community and its residents moved to Pukatawagan. These selected milestones describe changes in settlement and band administration within a much longer history of Cree life in the boreal north. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://mathiascolomb.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mathias Colomb — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Mathias Colomb \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://mathiascolomb.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mathias Colomb — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Separate-community milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1910 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://mathiascolomb.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mathias Colomb — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Prayer River survey\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 29, 1926 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://mathiascolomb.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mathias Colomb — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical river\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Churchill River \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://mathiascolomb.ca/about\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mathias Colomb — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "282": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Minegoziibe Anishinabe’s published community account traces its Saulteaux ancestors to the Lake Superior and Sault Ste. Marie region. Families made their home along the southwestern shore of Lake Winnipegosis, where fish, waterfowl and wild game supported community life. The account identifies the people as Plains Ojibwe or Anishinaabe and explains the French-derived Saulteaux name through its association with rapids. Pine Creek is another name encountered in records of the Nation. These accounts describe ancestry, movement and land-based life, rather than treating the establishment of a reserve as the beginning of Indigenous history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/minegoziibe-anishinabe/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Minegoziibe Anishinabe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Anishinaabe / Saulteaux \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/minegoziibe-anishinabe/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Minegoziibe Anishinabe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Pine Creek \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/minegoziibe-anishinabe/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Minegoziibe Anishinabe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Southwestern Lake Winnipegosis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/minegoziibe-anishinabe/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Minegoziibe Anishinabe — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "310": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Misipawistik Cree Nation’s history is closely connected with the Saskatchewan River rapids at Grand Rapids. The Lake Winnipeg Indigenous Collective describes how hydroelectric construction and the rerouting of the river in the 1960s left a dry riverbed where the rapids had been. This transformation changed an important feature of the community’s territory and its relationship with the water. The Collective’s Misipawistik perspectives project records community experience of these changes. The hydroelectric period is therefore a major part of the Nation’s recent history, while Cree relationships with the river and surrounding lands extend beyond the dam’s construction. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.lwic.org/misipawistik\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Misipawistik Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical place\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Grand Rapids \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.lwic.org/misipawistik\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Misipawistik Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Major landscape change\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Hydroelectric development in the 1960s \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.lwic.org/misipawistik\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Misipawistik Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>River\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Saskatchewan River \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.lwic.org/misipawistik\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Misipawistik Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "312": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Mosakahiken Cree Nation’s treaty history is recorded under Treaty 5. The federal treaty table lists its adhesion date as September 7, 1876. The Nation is also known through the Moose Lake place name, which appears in historical and community records. Its Treaty 5 relationship should be distinguished from later band administration and from the much longer Cree history of the region. These published records provide a confirmed treaty milestone, but do not establish an origin date for the people. No historical Chief or migration story is assigned here without a source connecting that detail specifically to Mosakahiken. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://epe.lac-bac.gc.ca/100/202/301/basic_departmental_inac/2003/bdd03_e.pdf?nodisclaimer=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosakahiken Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://epe.lac-bac.gc.ca/100/202/301/basic_departmental_inac/2003/bdd03_e.pdf?nodisclaimer=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosakahiken Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Adhesion date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 7, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://epe.lac-bac.gc.ca/100/202/301/basic_departmental_inac/2003/bdd03_e.pdf?nodisclaimer=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosakahiken Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical place name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Moose Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://epe.lac-bac.gc.ca/100/202/301/basic_departmental_inac/2003/bdd03_e.pdf?nodisclaimer=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Mosakahiken Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "313": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Nisichawayasihk Cree Nation locates its history where the Burntwood, Footprint and Rat rivers meet. Its own historical account emphasizes Nehetho language, storytelling and responsibilities to the ancestral homeland. Chief Pierre Moose and councillors Murdoch Hart and James Spence represented the community at the Treaty 5 adhesion ceremony at Nelson House on July 30, 1908. The Nation explains that its ancestors sought to safeguard their way of life and understood treaty-making through sharing and continuing relationships with the land. The treaty and reserve milestones belong within this much longer history, rather than defining its beginning. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ncncree.com/about-ncn/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nisichawayasihk Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 30, 1908 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ncncree.com/about-ncn/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nisichawayasihk Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Nelson House \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ncncree.com/about-ncn/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nisichawayasihk Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Pierre Moose \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ncncree.com/about-ncn/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nisichawayasihk Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical councillors\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Murdoch Hart and James Spence \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ncncree.com/about-ncn/our-history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Nisichawayasihk Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "317": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Northlands Denesuline’s community overview links its history with the earlier Barren Lands band at Brochet. The timeline records the Treaty 10 adhesion at Brochet on August 19, 1907, with Chief Petit Casimir among the signatories. Families began moving from Brochet to Lac Brochet in September 1973, building log homes at the new settlement. On March 21, 1978, Lac Brochet residents voted to divide the Barren Lands band and create Northlands. These events explain the relationship between the two present-day Nations. The band division was an administrative milestone within a much longer Denesuline history in the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teachforcanada.ca/en/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Northlands-Denesuline-Community-Overview-Apr-10-PAGES.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Northlands Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 10 adhesion at Brochet, 1907 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teachforcanada.ca/en/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Northlands-Denesuline-Community-Overview-Apr-10-PAGES.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Northlands Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Move to Lac Brochet\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1973 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teachforcanada.ca/en/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Northlands-Denesuline-Community-Overview-Apr-10-PAGES.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Northlands Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band-division vote\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>March 21, 1978 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teachforcanada.ca/en/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Northlands-Denesuline-Community-Overview-Apr-10-PAGES.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Northlands Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/barren-lands/\">Barren Lands\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Northlands was created through division of the earlier Barren Lands band. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teachforcanada.ca/en/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Northlands-Denesuline-Community-Overview-Apr-10-PAGES.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Northlands Denesuline First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "278": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Norway House Cree Nation’s history page places the community within the inland fur-trade transportation network. A post was built at Mossy Point in 1817, and the later post site was established in 1825–26. Norway House became an important Hudson’s Bay Company depot and meeting place. The Nation records the signing of Treaty 5 there on September 24, 1875. Its account also notes the development of Cree syllabics associated with James Evans and the designation of Norway House as a national historic site in 1932. These written milestones form part of the wider Cree history of the area. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nhcn.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Norway House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nhcn.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Norway House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 24, 1875 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nhcn.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Norway House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Early post\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Mossy Point, 1817 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nhcn.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Norway House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historic-site designation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1932 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://nhcn.ca/history/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Norway House Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "279": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">O-Chi-Chak-Ko-Sipi First Nation is associated with Crane River Reserve 51 on the northern basin of Lake Manitoba. A federal historical profile records its Treaty 2 relationship, dating to 1871, and identifies Ojibway as the community’s Indigenous language. The Crane River name provides a link between older reserve records and the present-day Nation. Reserve geography and treaty participation are documented milestones within a longer Anishinaabe history. The nearby non-reserve community has its own municipal history, so its incorporation dates should not be treated as the founding dates of the First Nation. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1994-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O-Chi-Chak-Ko-Sipi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 2, 1871 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1994-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O-Chi-Chak-Ko-Sipi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical reserve\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Crane River 51 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1994-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O-Chi-Chak-Ko-Sipi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ojibway \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1994-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O-Chi-Chak-Ko-Sipi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "318": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">O-Pipon-Na-Piwin Cree Nation’s history includes community efforts to restore land-based food knowledge at South Indian Lake. A research account describes how Elders and local food champions organized harvesting activities and training for young people. In 2012 they developed Ithinto Mechisowin, a year-round program whose name refers to food from the land. The program connects food, health, heritage and the renewal of community knowledge. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.tni.org/files/download/46_kamal_thompson_2013_0.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O-Pipon-Na-Piwin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> A Treaty Relations Commission report also records a July 2010 commemoration of the Nation’s July 2006 Treaty 5 adhesion. Together these accounts document treaty recognition and community-led cultural continuity. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://trcm.ca/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/2010-11-Annual-Report-Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty Relations Commission of Manitoba — 2010–11 annual report\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5, July 2006 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://trcm.ca/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/2010-11-Annual-Report-Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty Relations Commission of Manitoba — 2010–11 annual report\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land-based program\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ithinto Mechisowin, 2012 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.tni.org/files/download/46_kamal_thompson_2013_0.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O-Pipon-Na-Piwin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>South Indian Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.tni.org/files/download/46_kamal_thompson_2013_0.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: O-Pipon-Na-Piwin Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "315": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Opaskwayak Cree Nation’s educational authority describes a sustained movement toward community control of schooling. A 1979 study examined why students faced difficulty in the provincial system, identifying marginalization and cultural and language differences. Community consultation supported building a school and educating children on reserve. An alternative school opened in 1984 while negotiations continued for a kindergarten-to-Grade-12 school. This history records the work of families, educators and leadership to shape education around the needs of their children. It is one part of Opaskwayak’s broader history, told through the Nation’s own educational institution. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.opased.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/5-Year-Education-Strategic-Plan-Final-Draft.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Opaskwayak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Education study\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>tânêki, 1979 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.opased.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/5-Year-Education-Strategic-Plan-Final-Draft.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Opaskwayak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Alternative-school milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1984 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.opased.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/5-Year-Education-Strategic-Plan-Final-Draft.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Opaskwayak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical theme\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Community control of education \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.opased.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/5-Year-Education-Strategic-Plan-Final-Draft.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Opaskwayak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "327": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Pauingassi’s community history connects its name with nearby sand hills and recalls the area as a place for gathering and ceremony. Pimachiowin Aki records that Pauingassi and Little Grand Rapids were recognized within one band following the 1876 Treaty 5 adhesion. Pauingassi received reserve status in 1988 and became a separate First Nation in 1991, near the original village at Fishing Lake. The account also remembers Naamiwan, or Fair Wind, an Anishinaabe healer and leader. These milestones bring together cultural history and later administrative changes without reducing the community’s origins to the date of band recognition. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/pauingassi-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pauingassi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 adhesion, 1876 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/pauingassi-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pauingassi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve-status milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1988 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/pauingassi-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pauingassi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Separate First Nation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1991 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/pauingassi-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pauingassi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Naamiwan (Fair Wind) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/pauingassi-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pauingassi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/little-grand-rapids/\">Little Grand Rapids\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Pauingassi and Little Grand Rapids were administered as one band before Pauingassi became separate in 1991. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/pauingassi-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pauingassi First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "269": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Peguis First Nation is named for Chief Peguis, who led Saulteaux people from the Sault Ste. Marie region to Manitoba in the late eighteenth century. Treaty One’s community account identifies both Ojibway and Cree ancestry among its members. The Nation belongs to the Treaty 1 relationship, originating in the agreement signed at Lower Fort Garry on August 3, 1871. The history of Chief Peguis and the later treaty relationship represent different periods and should not be collapsed into a claim that he signed that treaty. These selected milestones provide context for the Nation’s name, ancestry and place in Manitoba’s history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/peguis-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peguis — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical leader\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Chief Peguis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/peguis-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peguis — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Ancestral movement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Sault Ste. Marie to Manitoba, late 1700s \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/peguis-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peguis — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 1 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/peguis-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Peguis — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "272": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Pinaymootang’s published community account connects Fairford with the earlier Partridge Crop place name. The area appears in fur-trade records through Duncan Cameron’s winter there in 1795–96 and John Richards McKay’s posting in 1846. A mission, store and church were established during the nineteenth century, and the Hudson’s Bay Company operated Fairford Post from 1871 until 1913. These are milestones in the written history of trade and colonial institutions at the settlement. They provide context for the Nation’s territory without treating the arrival of traders or missionaries as the beginning of Indigenous life there. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/pinay-motang/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pinaymootang First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical place name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Partridge Crop \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/pinay-motang/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pinaymootang First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Recorded trading visit\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1795–96 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/pinay-motang/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pinaymootang First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Fairford Post\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1871–1913 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/pinay-motang/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Pinaymootang First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "277": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Poplar River First Nation’s community account describes archaeological evidence of at least 7,000 years of continuous occupation in the area. Its leaders signed Treaty 5 on September 20, 1875. The Nation’s traditional territory extends east from Lake Winnipeg toward Ontario and is known as Asatiwisipe Aki. A community-based lands management plan became law in 2011, with the traditional lands designated a protected area. In 2018, the territories of Poplar River and three partner Nations became part of the Pimachiowin Aki World Heritage Site. The account links these milestones with continuing harvesting, fishing and family responsibilities to the land. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/poplar-river-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poplar River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty signing\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 20, 1875 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/poplar-river-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poplar River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional lands\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Asatiwisipe Aki \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/poplar-river-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poplar River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Lands-plan milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2011 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/poplar-river-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poplar River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>World Heritage inscription\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2018 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://pimaki.ca/about-us/communities/poplar-river-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Poplar River First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "300": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Red Sucker Lake’s treaty history includes the 1909 adhesion to Treaty 5. Four Arrows Regional Health Authority identifies the community’s language as the Island Lake Oji-Cree dialect. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/red-sucker-lake-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Sucker Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> The regional history also links Red Sucker Lake with Garden Hill, Wasagamack and St. Theresa Point through the earlier Island Lake band, which divided in 1969. These connections explain a shared administrative past while recognizing distinct present-day Nations. The treaty and band-division dates describe selected milestones, rather than the beginning of Anisininew history or of families’ relationships with the lakes and lands of the region. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/wasagamack-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Four Arrows — shared Island Lake band history\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 adhesion, 1909 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/red-sucker-lake-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Sucker Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Island Lake Oji-Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/red-sucker-lake-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Sucker Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Shared earlier band\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Island Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/red-sucker-lake-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Red Sucker Lake — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "291": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Rolling River First Nation’s history includes an enduring relationship with the Riding Mountain region. Parks Canada records Rolling River among the seven Nations participating in the Coalition of First Nations with Interests in Riding Mountain National Park. A ministerial agreement in 2006 established a forum for these Nations and Parks Canada to work together. The coalition addresses historical relationships with the area and supports the presentation of First Nation history and culture in the park. This is a documented modern institutional milestone within a much older relationship with the land, rather than a founding date for Rolling River. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/mb/riding/autochtones-indigenous-cultures/coalition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Rolling River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Riding Mountain \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/mb/riding/autochtones-indigenous-cultures/coalition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Rolling River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Coalition agreement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2006 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/mb/riding/autochtones-indigenous-cultures/coalition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Rolling River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Institution\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Coalition of First Nations with Interests in Riding Mountain National Park \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/mb/riding/autochtones-indigenous-cultures/coalition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Rolling River — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "273": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Roseau River Anishinaabe First Nation’s account through Treaty One locates its history in the Red River and Pembina valleys. It identifies the community with the Strong Heart People and with the Treaty 1 relationship established in 1871. The Nation’s later history includes a land-claim settlement in 2011 and the creation of a trust connected with that settlement. These milestones bring together cultural identity, treaty obligations and efforts to address historical land issues. They describe selected parts of Roseau River’s published history, while the community’s relationship with its territory and its oral traditions extend beyond the treaty and settlement dates. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/roseau-river-anishinaabe-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Roseau River Anishinabe First Nation Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 1 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/roseau-river-anishinaabe-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Roseau River Anishinabe First Nation Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Red River and Pembina valleys \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/roseau-river-anishinaabe-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Roseau River Anishinabe First Nation Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land-claim milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>2011 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/roseau-river-anishinaabe-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Roseau River Anishinabe First Nation Government — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "283": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sandy Bay Ojibway First Nation’s own account recalls the earlier White Mud band name and its distinction from the Portage band led by Chief Yellow Quill. It connects the community’s later settlement with the Treaty 1 era and records Nawachegapow as an early post-treaty Chief. The Nation’s lands are on the western shore of Lake Manitoba. Its account emphasizes the importance of keeping Ojibway language alive across generations. Some dates on the page are inconsistent, so this brief retains the documented names and broad treaty-era context without assigning a disputed exact date to the community’s establishment. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sbofn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sandy Bay — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier band name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>White Mud Band \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sbofn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sandy Bay — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Early recorded Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Nawachegapow \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sbofn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sandy Bay — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language heritage\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ojibway \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sbofn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sandy Bay — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Western Lake Manitoba \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sbofn.com/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sandy Bay — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "314": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sapotaweyak Cree Nation’s history appears in older records under the Shoal River name. A federal community profile records its Treaty 4 relationship, dating to 1874, and identifies Cree as the community’s Indigenous language. The Nation’s settlement is associated with Dawson Bay and the northern Lake Winnipegosis region, beside Pelican Rapids. Its reserve records retain the Shoal River name, linking historical documents with the present-day Nation. These published details provide a treaty and territorial framework for the brief; they do not establish the beginning of Cree life in the region or supply an unverified treaty-signing Chief. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1996-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sapotaweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1996-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sapotaweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Shoal River \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1996-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sapotaweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1996-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sapotaweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dawson Bay / Lake Winnipegosis \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-1996-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sapotaweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "303": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sayisi Dene history includes the forced relocation from the Little Duck Lake region to Churchill in 1956. Canada’s later apology acknowledged that the decision caused profound harm to the community and disrupted a way of life connected with the land and caribou. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1471286774906/1542900850824\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sayisi Dene First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> Families subsequently worked to return to a land-based community at Tadoule Lake in 1973. The federal government delivered its formal apology on August 16, 2016. These events are central to the community’s recent history of displacement, return and rebuilding. They form part of a much longer Sayisi Dene relationship with northern lands and waters. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1471286774906/1542900850824\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sayisi Dene First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sdfntrust.ca/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sayisi Dene First Nation Relocation Settlement Trust\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Forced relocation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1956 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1471286774906/1542900850824\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sayisi Dene First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Return to Tadoule Lake\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1973 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://sdfntrust.ca/about-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sayisi Dene First Nation Relocation Settlement Trust\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Federal apology\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 16, 2016 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1471286774906/1542900850824\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sayisi Dene First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "307": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Shamattawa First Nation’s treaty history includes an adhesion to Treaty 5 on August 10, 1910, as recorded in federal departmental data. This was later than the treaty’s original negotiations in the 1870s and should be described as an adhesion rather than an original 1875 signing. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://assembly.nu.ca/library/Edocs/2005/001219-e.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shamattawa First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> Keewatin Tribal Council places the community at the meeting of the Gods and Echoing rivers in northern Manitoba. These records provide a confirmed treaty milestone and geographical context. They do not establish a founding date for the community or justify assigning a historical Chief without a source connecting that person to Shamattawa. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/shamattawa-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keewatin Tribal Council\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://assembly.nu.ca/library/Edocs/2005/001219-e.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shamattawa First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Adhesion date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>August 10, 1910 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://assembly.nu.ca/library/Edocs/2005/001219-e.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Shamattawa First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical setting\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Gods and Echoing rivers \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/shamattawa-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Keewatin Tribal Council\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "290": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Sioux Valley Dakota Nation’s recent history includes a long effort to secure self-government. The Nation’s own account records the implementation of its self-government arrangements on July 1, 2014, following years of negotiations with Canada and Manitoba. The agreements created a framework for government-to-government relationships and for the Nation to exercise authority in community affairs. Sioux Valley identifies as Wipazoka Wakpa Oyate and grounds its government in Dakota identity and traditions. The 2014 milestone concerns the recognition and operation of modern governance arrangements; it does not mark the beginning of the people’s own laws or history. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://svdngovernance.com/a-self-governing-dakota-nation-new/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sioux Valley Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Dakota \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://svdngovernance.com/a-self-governing-dakota-nation-new/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sioux Valley Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Dakota name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Wipazoka Wakpa Oyate \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://svdngovernance.com/a-self-governing-dakota-nation-new/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sioux Valley Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Self-government implementation\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>July 1, 2014 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://svdngovernance.com/a-self-governing-dakota-nation-new/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Sioux Valley Dakota Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "281": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Skownan First Nation appears in older records as the Waterhen River Band of Saulteaux and later Waterhen First Nation. Its published community account identifies a Treaty 2 relationship and connects the Skownan name with an Anishinaabe expression referring to turning around a point. The community is associated with Waterhen Reserve 45 and the Waterhen Lake region. The older Waterhen name can cause confusion with Waterhen Lake First Nation in Saskatchewan, a separate Cree Nation. These names and treaty details help readers follow Skownan through historical records while recognizing its distinct Saulteaux identity and longer relationship with the land. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/skownan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Skownan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 2 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/skownan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Skownan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier band name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Waterhen River Band of Saulteaux \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/skownan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Skownan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical reserve\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Waterhen 45 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/skownan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Skownan First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "298": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">St. Theresa Point’s treaty history includes the 1909 adhesion to Treaty 5. Four Arrows Regional Health Authority identifies the local language as the Island Lake Oji-Cree dialect and places the community on the southwestern shore of Island Lake. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/st-theresa-point-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: St. Theresa Point — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a> The regional account links it with the earlier Island Lake band alongside Garden Hill, Wasagamack and Red Sucker Lake, until the division in 1969. These milestones explain the shared administrative history of the four present-day Nations. They form one part of the longer history of the Island Lake people and their relationships with the region’s lands and waters. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/garden-hill-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Four Arrows — Island Lake band history\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 adhesion, 1909 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/st-theresa-point-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: St. Theresa Point — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Island Lake Oji-Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/st-theresa-point-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: St. Theresa Point — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Shared earlier band\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Island Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/st-theresa-point-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: St. Theresa Point — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "293": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Swan Lake First Nation is an Anishinabe community whose published account identifies Saulteaux as its Indigenous language. It belongs to the Treaty 1 relationship established in 1871. Treaty One’s community profile describes a land base distributed among the main Swan Lake reserve, Carberry, Indian Gardens and Headingley. A treaty land entitlement settlement enabled the Nation to expand its lands for future community and economic development. This later land history is part of the continuing implementation of treaty obligations. It should be distinguished from the much older history of the people and from the dates of individual reserve additions. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/swan-lake-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 1 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/swan-lake-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Saulteaux \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/swan-lake-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical land process\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty land entitlement \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/swan-lake-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land-base locations\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Swan Lake, Carberry, Indian Gardens and Headingley \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty1.ca/swan-lake-first-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Swan Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "306": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Tataskweyak Cree Nation’s own environmental evaluation places its history within a continuing relationship with the homeland around Split Lake. The report records the 1908 adhesion to Treaty 5 and the earlier official names Split Lake Indian Band and Split Lake Cree Nation. It explains that ancestors approached treaty discussions through an understanding of sharing land and resources while continuing their way of life. The report also describes the profound changes in authority that followed colonial administration. This community-authored account provides a perspective on treaty history, rather than treating a government reserve designation as the beginning of Tataskweyak identity. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://aeic-iaac.gc.ca/050/documents_staticpost/64144/83657/Cree_-_01_Cree_Nations_Partners_Keeyask_Environmental_Evaluation_Report_Jan_2012.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tataskweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty adhesion\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5, 1908 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://aeic-iaac.gc.ca/050/documents_staticpost/64144/83657/Cree_-_01_Cree_Nations_Partners_Keeyask_Environmental_Evaluation_Report_Jan_2012.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tataskweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier recorded names\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Split Lake Indian Band; Split Lake Cree Nation \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://aeic-iaac.gc.ca/050/documents_staticpost/64144/83657/Cree_-_01_Cree_Nations_Partners_Keeyask_Environmental_Evaluation_Report_Jan_2012.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tataskweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Split Lake \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://aeic-iaac.gc.ca/050/documents_staticpost/64144/83657/Cree_-_01_Cree_Nations_Partners_Keeyask_Environmental_Evaluation_Report_Jan_2012.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tataskweyak Cree Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "292": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Tootinaowaziibeeng Treaty Reserve’s published community account identifies it as an Anishinabe Nation associated with Valley River Reserve 63A east of Roblin. The account places its history within a wider network of Ojibway communities around the Duck Mountain and Riding Mountain regions. It describes relationships with the earlier Swan River communities and other neighbouring Nations. This regional perspective helps explain why community histories extend beyond present reserve boundaries. The account is retained as the publishing organization’s description of those relationships; it is not used to assign a different numbered treaty or an unverified exact date to the Nation’s origins. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/tootinaowaiizeebeng/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tootinaowaziibeeng Treaty Reserve — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Anishinabe \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/tootinaowaiizeebeng/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tootinaowaziibeeng Treaty Reserve — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical reserve name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Valley River 63A \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/tootinaowaiizeebeng/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tootinaowaziibeeng Treaty Reserve — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical region\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Duck Mountain and Riding Mountain \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://treaty2.ca/tootinaowaiizeebeng/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Tootinaowaziibeeng Treaty Reserve — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "323": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">War Lake First Nation is associated with Ilford in northern Manitoba. Keewatin Tribal Council’s account describes Ilford’s development as a construction and service centre during the building of the Hudson Bay Railway. It later served as a marshalling point for prospectors during the Island Lake gold rush and for winter freight roads extending eastward. These milestones concern the written history of the settlement and transportation network. They should not be treated as the origin of Cree life in the area. The Nation’s own history and families’ relationships with the region extend beyond the railway-era description. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/war-lake-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: War Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ilford \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/war-lake-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: War Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Recorded historical role\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Hudson Bay Railway service centre \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/war-lake-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: War Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Later transportation role\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Winter freight-road marshalling point \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/war-lake-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: War Lake First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "299": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Wasagamack’s history includes the 1909 adhesion to Treaty 5 and the later division of the Island Lake band. Four Arrows Regional Health Authority records that Wasagamack, Garden Hill, St. Theresa Point and Red Sucker Lake remained part of one band until 1969. Wasagamack’s community is on the western shore of Island Lake, and its language is identified as the Island Lake Oji-Cree dialect. The shared band history explains relationships among the four Nations while preserving their distinct present-day identities. These treaty and administrative milestones are selected events within the longer history of the Island Lake people. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/wasagamack-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wasagamack First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 5 adhesion, 1909 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/wasagamack-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wasagamack First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Band division\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1969 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/wasagamack-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wasagamack First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Island Lake Oji-Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://fourarrowsrha.org/wasagamack-anishininew-nation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wasagamack First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "285": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Waywayseecappo First Nation’s traditional knowledge study, See What the Land Gave Us, documents relationships with lands and resources across generations. Prepared in 2017, the study draws on community interviews and earlier knowledge recorded in 1982. It connects historical land use with accounts of changes experienced over the following decades. The report identifies Waywayseecappo as an Ojibway community and a Treaty 4 Nation. This community-based record preserves a perspective that extends beyond reserve boundaries and administrative dates. It offers a starting point for understanding the Nation’s history through the experiences and knowledge of its own members. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/eal/registries/5950birtle/Proposal/appendixcwaywayseecappo1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waywayseecappo First Nation Treaty Four - 1874 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/eal/registries/5950birtle/Proposal/appendixcwaywayseecappo1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waywayseecappo First Nation Treaty Four - 1874 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>People\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Ojibway \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/eal/registries/5950birtle/Proposal/appendixcwaywayseecappo1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waywayseecappo First Nation Treaty Four - 1874 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Knowledge study\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>See What the Land Gave Us, 2017 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/eal/registries/5950birtle/Proposal/appendixcwaywayseecappo1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waywayseecappo First Nation Treaty Four - 1874 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier interview records\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1982 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/eal/registries/5950birtle/Proposal/appendixcwaywayseecappo1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Waywayseecappo First Nation Treaty Four - 1874 — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "324": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Wuskwi Sipihk First Nation appears in earlier records as Indian Birch. A federal community profile records its earlier connection with Sapotaweyak, or Shoal River, and its establishment as a separate reserve in September 1982. The profile identifies Cree as the Indigenous language and Treaty 4, signed in 1874, as the treaty relationship. Swan Lake Reserve 65C is associated with the Nation, distinct from the Swan Lake First Nation in southern Manitoba. These names and dates help distinguish the communities in archival records and explain Wuskwi Sipihk’s shared earlier administrative history with Sapotaweyak. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2001-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wuskwi Sipihk First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier name\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Indian Birch \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2001-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wuskwi Sipihk First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Separate-reserve milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 1982 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2001-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wuskwi Sipihk First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty 4, 1874 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2001-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wuskwi Sipihk First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cree \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2001-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wuskwi Sipihk First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory, events &amp; historical connections\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/sapotaweyak-cree-nation/\">Sapotaweyak Cree Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Wuskwi Sipihk was formerly part of Sapotaweyak (Shoal River), becoming separate in 1982. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/aanc-inac/R75-3-2001-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Wuskwi Sipihk First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These links reflect the relationship described by the cited account; each present-day Nation has its own identity and governance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>", "304": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">York Factory First Nation’s published history connects the people of York Landing with the Hayes River and the former Hudson’s Bay Company post at York Factory near Hudson Bay. Keewatin Tribal Council records that closure of the company’s store in 1957 was a major factor in the relocation to York Landing that year. The new community received reserve status in 1989. These changes linked fur-trade history with displacement and the establishment of a different settlement on the Nelson River. The relocation and reserve dates describe selected modern milestones within the people’s much longer Cree history in northern Manitoba. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/york-factory-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: York Factory First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Earlier community\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>York Factory, Hayes River \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/york-factory-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: York Factory First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Relocation to York Landing\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1957 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/york-factory-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: York Factory First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Reserve-status milestone\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>1989 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.ktc.ca/york-factory-first-nation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: York Factory First Nation — published history and community account\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Historical context\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">This brief covers selected published accounts. Community oral histories and knowledge extend beyond these records.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>"};
